INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(517,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11804624','DNA methylation-based profiling of uterine neoplasms: a novel tool to improve gynecologic cancer diagnostics.','Kommoss FKF, Stichel D, Schrimpf D, Kriegsmann M, Tessier-Cloutier B, Talhouk A, McAlpine JN, Chang KTE, Sturm D, Pfister SM, Romero-Pérez L, Kirchner T, Grünewald TGP, Buslei R, Sinn HP, Mechtersheimer G, Schirmacher P, Schmidt D, Lehr HA, Sahm F, Huntsman DG, Gilks CB, Kommoss F, von Deimling A, Koelsche C.','2019','infinium humanmethylation450 850k beadchip sne carcinosarcomas mmmt ulmo leiomyosarcomas ulms utrosct lgess hgess mmmt ulmo ulms utrosct lgess hgess hgess subgrouping ywhae utrosct kurman kandoth talhouk altrabulsi gremel cytosine esteller promotor somatically outnumbered sandoval esteller hovestadt koelsche pajtler rohrich capper talhouk quantifast sybr duesseldorf infinium humanmethylation450 450k 850k beadchip dkfz sne hierarchically sne perplexity maaten perplexities carcinosarcomas mmmt leiomyosarcomas ulms utrosct lgess hgess exonuclease lgess hgess utrosct histomorphology mmmt ulmo ulms utrosct lgess hgess ywhae nutm2 hgess ywhae nutm2 hgess zc3h7b hgess zc3h7b sne mmmt utrosct ulms lgess hgess hgess subgrouping ywhae hgess subgroupings sne carcinosarcomas mmmt utrosct ulmo leiomyosarcomas ulms lgess hgess altrabulsi gremel capper griewank koelsche rohrich segregates mmmt mmmt histogenetically mmmt metaplastic cherniack mccluggage mmmt mmmts mmmt chondroid rhabdomyoblastic mmmt bodelon ulms hgess q22 ywhae nutm2 hgess zc3h7b enriquez panagopoulos ulms hgess hgess hgess ywhae nutm2 hgess lgess ywhae hgess hgess zc3h7b hgess ywhae nutm2 hgess enriquez corepressor repressive prc1 utroscts kurman histogenesis utrosct kurman utroscts ncoa1 staats esr1 ncoa3 utroscts utrosct goebel utrosct adenosarcoma dkfz tgpg leopold ellinger avd fkfk fkfk fkfk avd dschr fkfk jnm ktc smp tgpg cbg dgh avd zeneca chugai zeneca medac smp avd avd idh1r132h brafv600e deimling koelsche altrabulsi malpica deavers bodurka broaddus bodelon killian matsuno brinton wentzensen lastra moroney veneris capper dtw sill hovestadt schrimpf nature26000 cherniack bowlby ccell cjr soslow colgan antonescu ncoa2 esteller methylomes nrg2005 yamaza ncb1913 assenov subero balint siebert esteller goebel bonilla hardisson kolin utrosct ncoa1 gremel hamzei hardell ghaderi ywhae fam22 griewank wiesner murali pischler koelsche sucker fibroxanthoma fat1 aneja conlon delair middha benayed mimicker hovestadt picelli kool northcott lichter nature13268 kandoth cherniack akbani nature12113 koelsche schrimpf stichel jabar ranft deimling s41379 koelsche kriegsmann kommoss fkf stichel kriegsmann vokuhl deimling ewsr1 nfatc2 kurman carcangiu herrington soslow delair murali hollmann zc3h7b enriquez lauria przybyl kowalewska debiec rychter mccluggage carcinosarcomas metaplastic pajtler sill hovestadt kratochwil johann pjcc ependymal ccell panagopoulos thorsen gorunova haugom bjerkehagen micci zc3h7b chromosom rohrich koelsche schrimpf capper sahm kratz reuss sandoval esteller gde shimamura shiraishi hoshino ncomms8557 staats santagata stubbs mccluggage utrosct jazf1 jjaz1 0b013e3181a7b9cf toprak hovestadt dtw capper kool talhouk mcconechy melnyk mcalpine maaten sne','Abstract Purpose Uterine neoplasms comprise a broad spectrum of lesions, some of which may pose a diagnostic challenge even to experienced pathologists. Recently, genome-wide DNA methylation-based classification of central nervous system tumors has been shown to increase diagnostic precision in clinical practice when combined with standard histopathology. In this study, we describe DNA methylation patterns of a diverse set of uterine neoplasms and test the applicability of array-based DNA methylation profiling. Methods A multicenter cohort including prototypical epithelial and mesenchymal uterine neoplasms was collected. Tumors were subject to pathology review and array-based DNA methylation profiling (Illumina Infinium HumanMethylation450 or EPIC [850k] BeadChip). Methylation data were analyzed by unsupervised hierarchical clustering and t-SNE analysis. Results After sample retrieval and pathology review the study cohort consisted of 49 endometrial carcinomas (EC), 5 carcinosarcomas (MMMT), 8 uterine leiomyomas (ULMO), 7 uterine leiomyosarcomas (ULMS), 15 uterine tumor resembling ovarian sex cord tumors (UTROSCT), 17 low-grade endometrial stromal sarcomas (LGESS) and 9 high-grade endometrial stromal sarcomas (HGESS). Analysis of methylation data identified distinct methylation clusters, which correlated with established diagnostic categories of uterine neoplasms. MMMT clustered together with EC, while ULMO, ULMS and UTROSCT each formed distinct clusters. The LGESS cluster differed from that of HGESS, and within the branch of HGESS, we observed a notable subgrouping of YWHAE - and BCOR -rearranged tumors. Conclusion Herein, we describe distinct DNA methylation signatures in uterine neoplasms and show that array-based DNA methylation analysis holds promise as an ancillary tool to further characterize uterine neoplasms, especially in cases which are diagnostically challenging by conventional techniques. Electronic supplementary material The online version of this article (10.1007/s00432-019-03093-w) contains supplementary material, which is available to authorized users. Keywords: Uterine neoplasms, DNA methylation profiling, Endometrial carcinoma, UTROSCT, Endometrial stromal sarcoma, Leiomyosarcoma Introduction Uterine neoplasms comprise a diverse group of lesions, with epithelial and mesenchymal tumors being the two largest and most frequently encountered tumor categories (Kurman et al. 2014 ). Endometrial adenocarcinoma (EC) is the most common uterine cancer and one of the most prevalent carcinomas in women of the western world (Siegel et al. 2019 ). Since EC are often clinically low-risk and present at an early stage, surgical treatment alone is often curative (Brooks et al. 2019 ). Uterine sarcomas (US) are much less common but clinically aggressive neoplasms with unfavorable prognosis, and treatment is often challenging. Recently, progress has been made in the classification of uterine neoplasms based on novel tumor-specific molecular findings. While for EC a molecular classification system with prognostic and predictive implications was established based on genomic architecture (Kandoth et al. 2013 ; Talhouk et al. 2015 ), US classification was improved through the identification of recurrent chromosomal translocations, e.g. in endometrial stromal sarcomas (ESS) (Hoang et al. 2018 ). While it is now possible to reliably diagnose most uterine neoplasms by histopathology alone or with the addition of immunohistochemical and molecular assays, some uterine tumors still lack unambiguous diagnostic traits and continue to pose a diagnostic challenge even to experienced pathologists (Altrabulsi et al. 2005 ; Gremel et al. 2015 ). During embryonic development, immature cells undergo differentiation by altering the transcriptional activity of specific genes through epigenetic mechanisms, such as DNA methylation (Shen and Laird 2013 ). The resulting cell lineage specific cytosine-5 methylation patterns are maintained through cell division, including during tumorigenesis (Esteller 2007 ). Hence, the promotor methylation patterns of cancer cells reflect properties of the originating precursor cell population. While there are also somatically acquired DNA methylation changes during cancer development, these are outnumbered by differentiation-specific patterns (Sandoval and Esteller 2012 ). These factors contribute to a tumor entity specific promoter methylation pattern (Fernandez et al. 2012 ). Recently, DNA methylation-based classification of tumors has been shown to be a reliable tool for defining tumor entities and to further improve cancer diagnostics (Hovestadt et al. 2014 ; Koelsche et al. 2018 , 2019 ; Pajtler et al. 2015 ; Rohrich et al. 2016 ; Sturm et al. 2016 ). The introduction of a DNA methylation-based classification system of central nervous system (CNS) tumors can reduce the substantial inter-observer variability observed in current CNS tumor diagnostics when combined with standard histology and molecular tumor classification (Capper et al. 2018 ). Here, we report specific DNA methylation signatures found in a diverse set of uterine neoplasms. Our findings indicate that DNA methylation analysis may help to better define subgroups of diagnostically challenging uterine tumors. Materials and methods Study cohort A large multicenter study cohort including epithelial and mesenchymal neoplasms of the uterine corpus was collected. The institutional diagnoses of all tumors included had previously been confirmed by expert pathology review in all cases. Additional molecular pathology, including RNA-seq and classification of EC according to the Proactive Molecular Risk Classifier for Endometrial Cancer (ProMisE) had previously been performed on a subset of tumors (Talhouk et al. 2015 ). DNA extraction and quantification DNA of all tumors was extracted from formalin-fixed paraffin-embedded (FFPE) tissue samples. All tumors included in this study had sufficient tumor material available to prevent extraction of neighboring benign tissue. Extracted DNA was quantified using the QuantiFast SYBR Green PCR Kit (Qiagen, Duesseldorf, NW, Germany). Genome-wide DNA methylation data generation In all cases a total amount of > 100 ng DNA was available for array-based DNA methylation analysis. Samples were analyzed using the Illumina Infinium HumanMethylation450 (450k) or EPIC (850k) BeadChip (Illumina, San Diego, USA), according to the manufacturer’s instructions at the Genomics and Proteomics Core Facility of the German Cancer Research Center (DKFZ) Heidelberg. DNA methylation data were normalized by performing background correction and dye bias correction. Probes targeting sex chromosomes, probes containing multiple single nucleotide polymorphisms and those, which could not be uniquely mapped, were removed. Unsupervised clustering and t-SNE analysis For unsupervised hierarchical clustering, the 10,000 probes with the highest median absolute deviation across beta-values were selected. Samples were hierarchically clustered using the Pearson correlation distance and Ward’s linkage method. To confirm the clusters identified we used t-distributed stochastic neighbor embedding (t-SNE) analysis, a method enabling dimensionality reduction and visualization of clusters. The algorithm was performed using the 10,000 most variable probes with a perplexity of 10 and 3000 iterations (van der Maaten and Hinton 2008 ). Methylation groups were tested for stability by varying the number of the most variable probes and perplexities. Results Study cohort After sample collection the study cohort consisted of a total of 110 uterine neoplasms, including 49 EC (Fig. 1 a), 5 carcinosarcomas with homologous, non-specific mesenchymal components (malignant mixed Mullerian tumors, MMMT, Fig. 1 b), 8 uterine leiomyomas (ULM; Fig. 1 c), 7 uterine leiomyosarcomas (ULMS; Fig. 1 d), 15 uterine tumors resembling ovarian sex cord tumors (UTROSCT; Fig. 1 e), 17 low-grade endometrial stromal sarcomas (LGESS; Fig. 1 f) and 9 high-grade endometrial stromal sarcomas (HGESS; Fig. 1 g–j). Previously performed institutional analysis classified EC according to the PRoMisE classifier, including 10 Polymerase-ɛ exonuclease domain mutated (POLE), 10 mismatch repair deficient (MMRd), 20 p53 wild-type (p53 wt) and 9 p53 mutated (p53 abn) tumors. RNA-sequencing identified pathognomonic gene fusion transcripts in a subset of LGESS, HGESS and UTROSCT (Dickson et al. 2019 ; Hoang et al. 2018 ). Clinicopathological and molecular characteristics of the studies cohort are summarized in supplementary material 1. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Representative histomorphology of tumors included in the study: a Endometrioid adenocarcinoma of the endometrium infiltrating the myometrium (EC), b carcinosarcoma (malignant mixed Mullerian tumor, MMMT) showing malignant epithelial and non-specific homologous component, c uterine leiomyoma (ULMO), d uterine leiomyosarcoma (ULMS) with extensive tumor cell necrosis, e uterine tumor resembling ovarian sex cord tumor (UTROSCT), f low-grade endometrial stromal sarcoma (LGESS), g high-grade endometrial stromal sarcoma (HGESS) with YWHAE - NUTM2 gene fusion showing multiple mitotic figures, h HGESS with YWHAE - NUTM2 gene fusion and tumor cell necrosis, i HGESS with ZC3H7B - BCOR gene fusion showing highly cellular areas and j HGESS with ZC3H7B - BCOR gene fusion and myxoid stromal change. Bar equals 50 µm Methylation clusters correlate with the histopathological diagnoses of differentiated neoplasms Unsupervised hierarchical clustering (Fig. 2 a) and t-SNE analysis (Fig. 2 b) identified clusters characterized by distinct DNA methylation patterns, which remained stable when varying the analyzed number of CpG sites and complexities. EC grouped together with MMMT in one DNA methylation cluster. UTROSCT, ULM and ULMS each formed a separate DNA methylation cluster. Similarly, LGESS and HGESS segregated into two distinct, subtype-specific DNA methylation cluster groups. In the HGESS cluster group we observed a subgrouping of YWHAE - and BCOR -rearranged HGESS, which remained stable when varying the number of CpG sites analyzed. Other subgroupings observed within other main methylation cluster groups did not remain stable when varying the number of CpG sites analyzed and did not correlate with tumor grade or molecular profiles of tumors. Supp. Figure 1 shows unsupervised hierarchical clustering of the study cohort including the methylation status of the 10,000 probes with the highest median absolute deviation across beta-values. Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Unsupervised hierarchical clustering ( a ) and t-distributed Stochastic Neighbor Embedding (t-SNE) analysis ( b ) using DNA methylation data of endometrial carcinomas (EC; n = 49), carcinosarcomas (malignant mixed Müllerian tumor, MMMT; n = 5), uterine tumors resembling ovarian sex cord tumors (UTROSCT; n = 15), uterine leiomyomas (ULMO; n = 8), uterine leiomyosarcomas (ULMS; n = 7), low-grade endometrial stromal sarcomas (LGESS; n = 17), and high-grade endometrial stromal sarcomas (HGESS; n = 9) Discussion Uterine neoplasms comprise a broad spectrum of benign and malignant lesions with a nonspecific clinical presentation in most cases. Adequate patient treatment is based on histopathological diagnosis. As some uterine neoplasms such as undifferentiated endometrial carcinoma and uterine sarcomas may pose a diagnostic challenge even to experienced pathologists (Altrabulsi et al. 2005 ; Gremel et al. 2015 ), it was the aim of our study to evaluate the use of DNA methylation profiling in a cohort of uterine tumors of different histotypes. Previous studies have shown DNA methylation profiling to be able to reliably assign CNS tumors to diagnostic groups, leading to the development of a methylation-based brain tumor classifier (Capper et al. 2018 ). Thus, DNA methylation profiling has evolved from merely being used as a research tool to a clinically applicable diagnostic test in neuropathology. Recently, the concept of establishing diagnostic categories through DNA methylation profiling has also been established in mesenchymal tumors of non-gynecological origin such as soft tissue sarcomas (Griewank et al. 2018 ; Koelsche et al. 2018 ; Rohrich et al. 2016 ; Seki et al. 2015 ). Similar results were obtained in our study demonstrating array-based DNA methylation analysis to be helpful in the classification of uterine tumors. Our DNA methylation data shows that EC segregates together, separate from other mesenchymal neoplasms, while showing similar DNA methylation patterns with MMMT. EC and MMMT are known to be closely related histogenetically as most studies suggest that MMMT represent poorly differentiated metaplastic adenocarcinomas, on the basis of morphological and molecular features shared with endometrioid and serous endometrial carcinoma as well as high-grade serous ovarian carcinomas (Cherniack et al. 2017 ; McCluggage 2002 ). Our current DNA methylation data adds further evidence supporting the concept of a common cell of origin of EC and MMMT. However, only MMMTs with homologous mesenchymal component were analyzed in the present study. Therefore, future studies should investigate if MMMT with heterologous sarcomatous components such as chondroid or rhabdomyoblastic morphology share methylation profiles with homologous MMMT and EC. Our current data show that the methylation profile of EC does not correlate with the molecular subtype of EC as defined by the ProMisE classifier (POLE, MMRd, p53 wt and p53 abn). Nevertheless, future methylation-based studies where methylation profile of EC is considered in the context of patient outcome could help further stratify EC clinically, as has recently been shown for ovarian cancer, where methylation-based classification of tumors added significant prognostic information (Bodelon et al. 2019 ). Mesenchymal uterine neoplasms such as ULMS and HGESS can show significant morphological overlap, and diagnosis can be challenging (L. N. Hoang et al. 2017 ). Our data show DNA methylation profiling to be able to distinguish between these closely related tumor types, thus showing potential to serve as a diagnostic adjunct in cases with inconclusive findings based on conventional assessment. According to the 2014 World Health Organization (WHO) classification of tumors of female reproductive organs only tumors with t(10;17)(q22;p13) chromosomal translocation resulting in a YWHAE - NUTM2 fusion are considered HGESS. Recently, a novel US entity with recurrent t(X;22)(p11;q13) resulting in ZC3H7B - BCOR gene fusion or a mutually exclusive somatic internal tandem duplication of exon 15 of BCOR has been described (Lewis et al. 2018 ; Mariño-Enriquez et al. 2018 ; Panagopoulos et al. 2013 ). Although such tumors show a significant morphological overlap with myxoid ULMS, they are considered to most likely represent HGESS (Hoang et al. 2017 ). This is supported by our results, which show that HGESS with BCOR -rearrangement and HGESS with YWHAE - NUTM2 gene fusion share similar yet distinct methylation profiles. Further, our data demonstrate that the methylation profiles of these HGESS subtypes also differ significantly from LGESS, although all are believed to be derived from endometrial stromal cells (Hoang et al. 2018 ). Nevertheless, the exact relationship between YWHAE -rearranged HGESS and HGESS with ZC3H7B - BCOR gene fusion remains elusive. Interestingly, the dysregulation of BCOR has been observed in both HGESS with YWHAE - NUTM2 gene fusion as well as in HGESS with BCOR -alterations (Chiang et al. 2017 ; Lewis et al. 2018 ; Mariño-Enriquez et al. 2018 ). BCOR acts as a transcriptional corepressor in the Polycomb repressive complex 1.1 (PRC1.1) and its dysregulation might add to a distinct DNA methylation signature in such cases (Fan et al. 2009 ). Further studies are needed to explore the oncogenic mechanisms of these BCOR-related changes, with a view to determine whether this can be a targeted therapeutically, as effective therapeutic options, beyond surgery, do not exist for these aggressive tumors. UTROSCTs are rare uterine neoplasms which resemble ovarian sex cord tumors and show a benign clinical course in most cases (Kurman et al. 2014 ). Although the exact histogenesis of UTROSCT remains controversial, these tumors are currently listed under the category “Endometrial Stromal and Related Tumors” in the current WHO classification (Kurman et al. 2014 ). While characteristic gene fusions of stromal neoplasms have never been identified in UTROSCTs, genomic studies have only recently uncovered recurrent NCOA1 - 3 rearrangement in such tumors (Dickson et al. 2019 ; Staats et al. 2009 ). Analysis by RNA-seq identified ESR1 - NCOA3 gene fusion transcripts in two UTROSCTs included in our study cohort. Our data supports the classification of UTROSCT as a distinct tumor entity, which in addition to the sex cord-like morphology and the expression of sex cord markers is defined by a distinct DNA methylation signature and recurrent gene fusions (Goebel et al. 2019 ). Therefore, DNA methylation profiling could help in distinguishing UTROSCT from potential mimics such as endometrial stromal neoplasms with sex cord-like differentiation. Because our collective has covered a broad, but not comprehensive spectrum of uterine neoplasms (e.g. adenosarcoma), further epigenetic-based studies are warranted. Conclusion This study describes DNA methylation profiles of the most common uterine tumor types, as well as some rare uterine neoplasms and indicates its usefulness in gynecologic cancer diagnostics. We believe that an integrative approach using histology alongside epi-/genetic profiling improves diagnostic precision, especially in those tumors with undifferentiated histological appearance and nonspecific genetic alterations. Electronic supplementary material Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. Supplementary material 1 (XLSX 14 kb) (14.4KB, xlsx) Acknowledgements We want to thank the Microarray Unit of the Genomics and Proteomics Core Facility, German Cancer Research Center (DKFZ) for providing excellent technical support. This work was supported by the German Cancer Aid (Grant: 70112499). The laboratory of TGPG was supported by the Dr. Leopold and Carmen Ellinger foundation and the German Cancer Aid (Grant: 70112257). Author contributions AvD, FKFK and CK conceptualized the project, FKFK and CK wrote the original draft. FKFK and CK coordinated data generation. CK, FS and AvD supervised RNA sequencing and DNA methylation array analysis, DSchr and DSt analyzed data. FKFK, MK, BT-C, AT, JNM, KTC, DS, SMP, LR-P, TK, TGPG, RB, HS, GM, PS, DS, HL, CBG, DGH, FK, AvD and CK provided tumor samples and corresponding metadata. All authors reviewed and approved of the final manuscript. Compliance with ethical standards Conflict of interest PS: grants from MSD, BMS, Roche, Astra Zeneca, Novartis, Sanofi-Aventis, Chugai, Illumina and Thermo Fisher; personal fees from MSD, BMS, Roche, Astra Zeneca, AbbVie, Ipsen and Novartis. FS: lecturing honoraria from Illumina, Medac and Agilent; board member of AbbVie. SMP and AvD: patent pending for a “DNA-methylation based method for classifying tumors”. AvD: issued patent for IDH1R132H and BRAFv600E antibodies. All other authors declare no conflict of interest. Ethical approval This study was performed in accordance with the ethical standards of the institutional research committee and the Declaration of Helsinki. Footnotes Publisher''s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. Andreas von Deimling and Christian Koelsche contributed equally. References Altrabulsi B, Malpica A, Deavers MT, Bodurka DC, Broaddus R, Silva EG (2005) Undifferentiated carcinoma of the endometrium. Am J Surg Pathol 29(10):1316–1321 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Bodelon C, Killian JK, Sampson JN, Anderson WF, Matsuno R, Brinton LA, Wentzensen N (2019) Molecular classification of epithelial ovarian cancer based on methylation profiling: evidence for survival heterogeneity. Clin Cancer Res. 10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-18-3720 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Brooks RA, Fleming GF, Lastra RR, Lee NK, Moroney JW, Son CH, Veneris JL (2019) Current recommendations and recent progress in endometrial cancer. CA Cancer J Clin 69(4):258–279. 10.3322/caac.21561 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Capper D, Jones DTW, Sill M, Hovestadt V, Schrimpf D, Sturm D, Pfister SM (2018) DNA methylation-based classification of central nervous system tumours. Nature 555(7697):469–474. 10.1038/nature26000 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Cherniack AD, Shen H, Walter V, Stewart C, Murray BA, Bowlby R, Levine DA (2017) integrated molecular characterization of uterine carcinosarcoma. Cancer Cell 31(3):411–423. 10.1016/j.ccell.2017.02.010 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Chiang S, Lee CH, Stewart CJR, Oliva E, Hoang LN, Ali RH, Soslow RA (2017) BCOR is a robust diagnostic immunohistochemical marker of genetically diverse high-grade endometrial stromal sarcoma, including tumors exhibiting variant morphology. Mod Pathol 30(9):1251–1261. 10.1038/modpathol.2017.42 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Dickson BC, Childs TJ, Colgan TJ, Sung YS, Swanson D, Zhang L, Antonescu CR (2019) Uterine tumor resembling ovarian sex cord tumor: a distinct entity characterized by recurrent NCOA2/3 gene fusions. Am J Surg Pathol 43(2):178–186. 10.1097/PAS.0000000000001153 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Esteller M (2007) Cancer epigenomics: DNA methylomes and histone-modification maps. Nat Rev Genet 8(4):286–298. 10.1038/nrg2005 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Fan Z, Yamaza T, Lee JS, Yu J, Wang S, Fan G, Wang CY (2009) BCOR regulates mesenchymal stem cell function by epigenetic mechanisms. Nat Cell Biol 11(8):1002–1009. 10.1038/ncb1913 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Fernandez AF, Assenov Y, Martin-Subero JI, Balint B, Siebert R, Taniguchi H, Esteller M (2012) A DNA methylation fingerprint of 1628 human samples. Genome Res 22(2):407–419. 10.1101/gr.119867.110 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Goebel EA, Hernandez Bonilla S, Dong F, Dickson BC, Hoang LN, Hardisson D, Kolin DL (2019) Uterine tumor resembling ovarian sex cord tumor (UTROSCT): a morphologic and molecular study of 26 cases confirms recurrent NCOA1-3 rearrangement. Am J Surg Pathol. 10.1097/pas.0000000000001348 (Epub ahead of print) [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubM