an Federation of Neurological Scoieties . Recommended core curriculum for a specialist training program in neurology. Eur J Neurol. 2005;12:743‐746. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Rakusa M, Rot U. Toward a unified European curriculum—reforming national residency programme in Slovenia. Eur J Neurol. 2020;27:e80. doi: 10.1111/ene.14441 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Kleineberg NN, van der Meulen M, Franke C, et al. Differences in neurology residency training programmes across Europe—a survey among the residents and research fellow section of the European Academy of Neurology national representatives. Eur J Neurol. 2020;27:1356‐1363. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Sauerbier A, Macerollo A, Györfi O, et al. Insufficient global health education in European neurological post‐graduate training: a European Association of Young Neurologists and Trainees survey. Eur J Neurol. 2016;23:1680‐1683. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Struhal W, Mellgren SI, Grisold W. Three important steps to European neurology harmonization: core curriculum, visitation program. European Board Examination Eur J Neurol. 2013;20:101‐104. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Györfi O, Moarcas M, Balicza P, Macerollo A, Struhal W, Sellner J. European junior neurologists perceive various shortcomings in current residency curricula. Acta Neurol Scand. 2016;134:232‐237. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Rakusa M, Sieminski M, Rakusa S, et al. Awakening to sleep disorders in Europe: survey on education, knowledge and treatment competence of European residents and neurologists. Eur J Neurol. 2021;28:2863‐2870. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Frank JR. The CanMEDS 2005 Physician Competency Framework: Better Standards, Better Physicians, Better Care. Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada; 2005. [ Google Scholar ] 26. Bassetti CL, Hess CW, Steck A, Ludin HP. A 110‐year history of the Swiss Neurological Society (SNG) through the biosketches of its first 42 presidents. Clinical and Translational Neurosci. 2018;2:30. doi: 10.1177/2514183X18788797 [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Bassetti CLA, Endres M, Sander A, et al. The European Academy of Neurology Brain Health Strategy: One brain, one life, one approach. Eur J Neurol. 2022;29:2559‐2566. doi: 10.1111/ene.15391 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 28. Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada . Standards of Accreditation for Residency Programs in Adult and Paediatric Neurology; 2020:145A/145P. [ Google Scholar ] 29. Tanner FC, Brooks N, Fox KF, et al. ESC core curriculum for the cardiologist. Europ Heart J. 2020;41:3605‐3691. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 30. Smit MP, de Hoog M, Brackel HJL, Ten Cate O, Gemke RJBJ. A national process to enhance the validity of entrustment decisions for Dutch pediatric residents. Med Educ. 2019;11:158‐164. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 31. ten Cate OT, Carraccio C. Envisioning a true continuum of competency‐based medical education, training, and practice. Acad Med. 2019;94:1283‐1288. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 32. ten Cate O. Nuts and bolts of entrustable professional activities. J Grad Med Educ. 2013;5:157‐158. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 33. Lucey CR, Thibault GE, Ten Cate O. Competency‐based, time‐variable education in the health professions: crossroads. Acd Med. 2018;93 (3S Competency‐Based, Time‐Variable Education in the Health Professions):S1‐S5. doi: 10.1097/ACM.0000000000002080 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement Data sharing is not applicable to this article as no new data were created or analyzed in this study.',2,'2025-10-08 11:19:34','2025-10-08 11:19:34');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(596,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12049957','Reduction of inappropriate perioperative neurology outpatient referrals for perioperative risk assessment and antithrombotic risk management in a major academic hospital.','Ng Si Min B, He Y, Pang YZ, Kok Jie Yi E, Goh ZH, Setiawan F, Jappar I, De Silva DA, Abdullah HRB.','2025','antithrombotics postponements roadshow postponements antithrombotics transcardiac hypercoagulability covert covert counselled antithrombotics antithrombotics antithrombotics embarked postponements postimplementation postponements fishbone fishbone pareto multivoting brainstormed pareto intranet roadshow yzp bnsm preimplementation postimplementation preimplementation postimplementation postimplementation postponements postponements postponements preimplementation postimplementation postponements postponements postponements postimplementation postponements annum intranet standardising nkl yzp sxxs benesch derdeyn nonneurological e923 hallqvist aburahma avgerinos 26s 98s fanning bcv bulbulia s41572 mrkobrada mtv covert neurovision','Abstract Perioperative stroke, a rare but serious complication, refers to any thrombotic, embolic or haemorrhagic cerebrovascular event lasting more than 24 hours, occurring intraoperatively or within 30 days of surgery. Preoperative assessment is critical for identifying high-risk patients and mitigating stroke risks. Despite established guidelines, a lack of standardised protocols at our institution led to inappropriate neurology referrals and unnecessary suspension of antithrombotics. At our preoperative evaluation clinic, which assesses 100–120 patients daily, challenges arose in managing patients at risk of perioperative stroke. An analysis from April to September 2022 revealed that 83.3% of 24 neurology referrals were inappropriate, causing surgical delays due to medicolegal concerns, surgery postponement fears and unclear guidelines. To address this, a quality improvement project was launched to reduce inappropriate neurology referrals by 15% in 6 months. The secondary aim was to create standardised management guidelines and evaluate both the incidence of surgical postponements and perioperative stroke outcomes. The project employed the plan–do–study–act framework, with interventions including the development of easily accessible standardised protocols and staff education through roadshow education platforms. Postintervention analysis showed a statistically significant 16.6% reduction in inappropriate neurology referrals (p=0.04), surpassing the 15% reduction target. Surgical postponements due to preoperative neurology consultations also decreased. There were no perioperative strokes reported during the project period, ensuring that the patient safety was not compromised. Additionally, the intervention resulted in cost savings of approximately $2134 annually by avoiding unnecessary consultations. This project highlights the effectiveness of multidisciplinary collaboration in reducing inappropriate neurology referrals and improving perioperative stroke risk management. The standardised guidelines have streamlined workflows and enhanced patient care, with sustained efforts planned to ensure long-term adherence to these protocols. Future directions include expanding the implementation of standardised protocols to other specialties within the institution. Keywords: Anaesthesia, Clinical Protocols, Evidence-Based Practice, Quality improvement WHAT IS ALREADY KNOWN ON THIS TOPIC Perioperative stroke, though rare, is a serious complication, with inconsistent application of guidelines across institutions leading to unnecessary neurology referrals and suspension of antithrombotics. WHAT THIS STUDY ADDS This study shows that implementing standardised protocols and educational initiatives can reduce inappropriate neurology referrals by 16.6%, reduce surgical delays and maintain patient safety. HOW THIS STUDY MIGHT AFFECT RESEARCH, PRACTICE OR POLICY These findings highlight the benefits of streamlined perioperative stroke risk management, which may guide other institutions in adopting similar protocols and promoting policy changes to enhance patient care and reduce healthcare costs. Introduction Perioperative stroke is defined as any thrombotic, embolic or haemorrhagic cerebrovascular event of a duration of more than 24 hours that occurs intraoperatively or within 30 days of a surgical operation. 1 Potential mechanisms for perioperative stroke include hypotension and other global low-flow states, large artery stenosis, tissue hypoxia related to anaemia, thromboembolism (cardiac or transcardiac), fat embolism and thrombosis/hypercoagulability in the context of provoking factors, such as systemic inflammation, endothelial dysfunction or cessation of antithrombotic medications. 1 Background The prevalence of perioperative stroke varies based on the surgery type and patient population. For non-cardiac, non-neurological surgeries, the incidence of clinically overt perioperative stroke ranges from 0.1% to 0.7%, with ischaemic strokes more common than haemorrhagic strokes. The Society for Vascular Surgery reports perioperative stroke risks ranging from 0.08% to 3.0%, depending on the surgery type. 2 , 4 Covert strokes, which are asymptomatic but detected on imaging, occur more frequently, with a 7% incidence rate in non-cardiac surgeries. Though not immediately apparent clinically, covert strokes can lead to significant long-term cognitive decline and increase the risk of future cerebrovascular events. 1 5 Preoperative assessment aims to identify patients at higher risk of perioperative stroke so that measures can be taken to mitigate these risks. Patients can also be accurately counselled on their perioperative risk profile, facilitating informed decision-making. The American Heart Association/American Stroke Association stresses the importance of careful preoperative evaluation to mitigate stroke risk in surgical patients. This process includes reviewing the patient’s medical history and risk factors, such as cardiovascular health, prior stroke or transient ischaemic attack, carotid artery disease and atrial fibrillation. 1 Other major society guidelines also provide recommendations on the timing of surgical procedures following strokes and the perioperative management of antithrombotics and anticoagulants. 3 Despite established international guidelines, our institution’s lack of a standardised protocol frequently results in unnecessary suspension of antithrombotics or inappropriate neurology referrals. Local problem Our institution’s preoperative evaluation clinic (PEC) assesses between 100 and 120 patients a day for preoperative assessment and optimisation ahead of elective surgery. Many of these patients have histories of previous stroke with the use of antithrombotics or anticoagulants. The team identified several challenges in managing patients at risk of perioperative stroke. These included unnecessary or prolonged suspension of antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy, lack of familiarity with perioperative stroke management guidelines and inappropriate outpatient preoperative neurology referrals. Analysis of electronic health records from April to September 2022 revealed that 83.3% of the 24 preoperative neurology referrals were inappropriate, resulting in unnecessary surgical delays and longer waiting times. Causes of inappropriate referrals included medicolegal concerns, fear of postponing surgeries and the absence of standardised guidelines. The team embarked on a quality improvement (QI) project to address the above issues by reducing inappropriate preoperative neurology outpatient referrals for perioperative risk assessment and antithrombotic risk management. The primary aim was to identify root causes and decrease inappropriate preoperative referrals to neurology by 15% within 6 months, using specific, measurable, achievable, realistic, time-bound criteria. This goal was specific to reducing unnecessary referrals to neurology, measurable through postintervention referral rates, achievable within the 6-month timeframe and realistic at a 15% reduction. The secondary aim was to create standardised management guidelines for our institution. We also seek to measure the incidence of surgical postponements pre- and postimplementation. The balancing outcome measure was the incidence of perioperative stroke. Methodology Design The QI project was structured based on the plan–do–study–act cycle, a systematic four-stage problem-solving model to test and implement changes to improve processes or outcomes. Problem analysis The team performed a baseline analysis of PEC electronic health records from April to September 2022. There were a total of 24 preoperative neurology referrals within this 6-month period. Following discussion with the neurology department, referrals were considered appropriate if a stroke had occurred within 6 months preceding the referral or if there was known significant intracranial vascular stenosis, and inappropriate if neither of these criteria were fulfilled. Based on these criteria, 20 of 24 referrals (83.3%) were inappropriate. Outcome measure The primary outcome measure was the number of inappropriate preoperative neurology referrals. Referrals were considered inappropriate if the occurrence of stroke was more than 6 months preceding the referral or if there was no significant intracranial stenosis, defined as any flow-limiting stenosis or >50% stenosis seen on cross-sectional angiographic imaging. Physician representatives from both departments of anaesthesia and neurology assessed the appropriateness of referrals made during two 6-month periods immediately preceding and following intervention. The intervention was implemented in September 2022, and the analysis included records from April 2022 to March 2023. The secondary outcome measure was the incidence of surgery postponement, with a specific focus on delays related to preoperative neurology referrals. Long waiting times for neurology appointments made postponement of surgeries inevitable if preoperative referrals were made. The data on day-of-surgery postponements due to incomplete neurological preoperative evaluation were collected for the postintervention time period, but similar data were not available from the preintervention time period. Root cause analysis The team engaged relevant stakeholders from anaesthesiology, surgical and neurology teams to identify the root causes of inappropriate referrals using the fishbone diagram ( figure 1 ), which was categorised into patient, doctor and process. The root causes were identified through collaborative discussions with a multidisciplinary team, including the heads of the anaesthesiology and surgical departments. Data were collected via surveys to obtain feedback from anaesthesiology and surgical staff, as well as through workflow observations to detect potential inefficiencies. The key factors contributing to high rates of outpatient perioperative neurology referral include a lack of clear perioperative guidelines on the timeline of performing elective surgery after a major stroke, antiplatelet and antithrombotic management and risk counselling. Furthermore, clinicians’ potentially defensive practices also contribute to the unnecessarily high referral rate. Finally, patient’s anxiety about having a perioperative stroke also contributed to the high referral rates. Figure 1. Fishbone diagram. Open in a new tab Next, a Pareto chart ( figure 2 ) was constructed to identify three main root causes to aid us in prioritising the appropriate interventions to achieve our target outcome. Multivoting was used to rank each of these root causes. The three main root causes identified were: (1) fear of medical-legal issues; (2) concerns about the postponement of surgery and (3) lack of clear guidelines available. Based on the root causes identified, a driver diagram and prioritisation matrix ( figure 3 ) were created, where the team brainstormed solutions to address the root causes. The best solutions were identified using a scoring system. The criteria for the scoring system that the team agreed on were: (1) time saving; (2) cost saving; (3) feasibility and (4) sustainability. Figure 2. Pareto chart to identify root causes. Open in a new tab Figure 3. Tree diagram and prioritisation matrix for solution selection. Open in a new tab Finally, the team identified that an appropriate and feasible intervention would be to ensure that formal institution guidelines on this topic are made available and accessible to all relevant stakeholders, including surgical teams. Two solutions, standardised protocols and staff education, were selected for implementation. The team determined that an effective and viable intervention would involve ensuring widespread accessibility of formal institutional guidelines on this subject to all pertinent stakeholders, including surgical teams. The team opted to implement two solutions: standardised protocols and staff education. Interventions Standardised protocols Standardised protocols for those with previous ischaemic stroke were instituted and made easily available on the hospital intranet, helping stakeholders with risk stratification, determining suitable timing of surgery in relation to prior stroke and appropriate durations of antiplatelet/anticoagulation cessation (Annex 1). These guidelines were created with reference to the scientific statement from ‘Perioperative Neurological Evaluation and Management to Lower the Risk of Acute Stroke in Patients Undergoing Non-Cardiac, Non-Neurological Surgery: A Scientific Statement From the American Heart Association/American Stroke Association’. 1 The guidelines were created together in discussion and collaboration with Singapore General Hospital’s neurology, cardiology and anaesthesiology department. This easy-to-reference guideline was selected for its feasibility and convenience. Guidelines are also easier to standardise and distribute to a larger target group of anaesthesiology, surgical and neurology teams. Staff education This solution involved educating both the anaesthesiology preoperative doctors and the various surgical specialties. In September 2022, the team conducted roadshow education platforms to raise awareness of the guidelines among the various departments. First, the QI Project Leads, YH and YZP, met with the respective Service Chiefs within Singapore General Hospital’s Division of Surgery and Surgical Oncology, Division of Musculoskeletal Sciences and Division of Perioperative Sciences and Anaesthesiology. These meetings included all surgical specialties and focused on sharing and educating the departments on the new perioperative guidelines. Subsequently, Quality Project Team Member BNSM delivered a continuing medical education lecture to the Division of Perioperative Sciences and Anaesthesiology, further emphasising the new guidelines and raising awareness among the anaesthesiology junior doctors who review patients in the preoperative clinic. Finally, the Singapore General Hospital pharmacy department distributed a pharmacy bulletin to the Division of Perioperative Sciences and Anaesthesiology, providing easy-to-reference summarised guidelines on perioperative management of antithrombotic and antiplatelet medications. Data collection and analysis To assess the impact of our intervention, the team decided to analyse the appropriateness of perioperative neurology referrals. Referrals made to the neurology clinic from electronic medical records during the preimplementation (April–September 2022) and postimplementation (October 2022–March 2023) time periods were screened. Categorical data were presented as numbers (percentages) and analysed using an independent t-test to determine significant differences in the percentage of inadequate referrals between the preintervention and postintervention phases. Statistical significance was defined as p<0.05. Results Over 12 months, a total of 42 preoperative referrals to the Department of Neurology were made, with 24 occurring preintervention and 18 postintervention. From April to September 2022 (preimplementation), 20 out of 24 referrals (83.3%) were inappropriate. From October 2022 to March 2023 (postimplementation), 12 out of 18 referrals (66.7%) were inappropriate. An independent t-test assessing the appropriateness of perioperative neurology referrals pre- and postimplementation was performed, using a significance level of p<0.05. The results show a statistically significant 16.6% (p=0.04) reduction in inappropriate referrals after our interventions, exceeding the target of a 15% reduction within 6 months. At our institution, patients requiring preoperative neurology consultations often faced surgical postponements due to prolonged wait times in the neurology clinic, leading to the rescheduling of surgeries after the neurology assessment. Following the intervention, the rate of surgical postponements decreased, with 24 postponements recorded preintervention and 18 postintervention. Between April and September 2022 (preimplementation), 24 out of 12 309 patients (0.0019%) experienced surgery delays due to preoperative neurology referrals. From October 2022 to March 2023 (postimplementation), 18 out of 11 459 patients (0.0016%) had their surgeries postponed for the same reason. No same-day surgical postponements due to missing or inappropriate preoperative neurology referrals were reported during the project period. In the year prior (February 2021–March 2022), there were a total of three perioperative strokes identified. There were no stroke incidences seen within the project’s time period of April 2022–March 2023. Singapore General Hospital charges an average of $133.33 per specialist outpatient visit, saving an approximate $133.33 per patient. Over the 6-month study, avoiding eight inappropriate referrals resulted in a total cost savings of $1067, equivalent to $2134 annually. Discussion The team evaluated the root causes of inappropriate preoperative referrals. The analysis identified key factors: fear of medical-legal issues, concerns about surgical delays and unclear guidelines. This QI project demonstrates a significant reduction in inappropriate referrals to the neurology department for perioperative risk assessment and antithrombotic management following our interventions. The team successfully achieved the primary goal of reducing inappropriate referrals. We successfully met the secondary objective of developing standardised management guidelines for our institution. This achievement ensures the delivery of more consistent, evidence-based care, reducing variability in practice and minimising the risk of treatment delays or unnecessary medical-legal complications. An audit on surgical postponements due to the lack of preoperative referrals demonstrated a reduction in postponements postimplementation. These findings highlight that our interventions improved operational efficiency without negatively impacting surgical scheduling or patient outcomes. This reduction in delays underscores the intervention’s role in streamlining workflows—essential for optimising patient care and resource management in healthcare settings. Additionally, there were no same-day surgical postponements during the study period due to incomplete or inappropriate neurology referrals, further supporting the reliability of the intervention. The balancing outcome measures further underscore the project’s success. The absence of perioperative strokes both before and after the intervention suggests that the new guidelines and the reduced preoperative neurology referrals did not compromise patient safety. For patient savings, an approximate $2134 per annum saved. Avoiding unnecessary consultations and tests can lead to significant cost savings for healthcare systems and patients, as resources are not wasted on consultations and investigations that may not contribute significantly to patient care outcomes. The patients would benefit from a streamlined process that avoids unnecessary appointments and tests, reducing stress and inconvenience associated with unnecessary medical consultations or investigations. This project also highlighted the importance of multidisciplinary collaborations at an interhospital level. The early formation of a multidisciplinary team among Singapore General Hospital’s anaesthesiology, neurology and cardiology department allowed us to work with clinical experts and standardise the practice with international guidelines. This facilitates the provision of better and safer care in an evidence-based manner. Good interdepartmental sharing and collaboration for improvement of workflows contributed to greater cooperation between our local tertiary hospital’s anaesthesiology and surgical departments as well. The improved communication and cooperation between departments can potentially enhance care coordination and patient outcomes across the institution. The continued support from anaesthesiology, neurology and surgical leadership as well as the guidance of the QI coaches contributed greatly to the success of the project as well. Finally, this intervention may also result in benefits that extend beyond the Department of Neurology. The introduction of standardised protocols and staff education may have broader implications for reducing inappropriate referrals in other medical specialties, amplifying the impact of this project beyond neurology. Challenges faced The team faced challenges during the project. There was initially reduced awareness of the guidelines, with staff rotating to different departments and having new doctors joining over time. To overcome this, regular multidisciplinary sessions with anaesthesiology and surgical specialities were conducted to raise awareness of these guidelines. The guidelines were also made readily and easily accessible on the intranet. The anaesthesiology department regularly reminded their physicians, via platforms such as department meetings. Regular feedback was obtained through interdepartmental sharing and with collaborations for improvement of workflow. Limitations The team had a small sample size of 42 referrals over a period of 24 months. Such a small sample size may not be sufficient to detect a statistical difference in this pre- and post-QI design, which is concerning for type II error. There was also a limited period of observation. Subsequently, no formal survey was conducted to patients who did not require preoperative neurology evaluation. Hence, no formal feedback was collected to understand their views regarding preoperative neurology assessment. The initial version of our standardised guidelines did not incorporate the 2022 CHEST guidelines regarding anticoagulation management and considerations for low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) bridging. These guidelines have since been revised to include the CHEST 2022 recommendations and factors to assess prior to initiating bridging therapy with LMWH. Sustainability measures and future directions The team will make these standardised guidelines available and accessible to all relevant stakeholders. The team will also conduct regular multidisciplinary sessions through department teachings to increase the awareness of these guidelines. Conclusion In conclusion, this QI project successfully addressed the root causes of inappropriate preoperative referrals to the neurology department by implementing targeted interventions. Key factors contributing to inappropriate referrals—fear of medical-legal issues, concerns about surgical delays and unclear guidelines—were mitigated through the creation of standardised management guidelines. The project achieved its primary goal of reducing inappropriate referrals and its secondary aim of the creation of guidelines and standardising care for antiplatelet and antithrombotic management. Importantly, patient safety was maintained, as evidenced by the absence of perioperative strokes and no postponement of surgeries due to reduced preoperative referrals. Cost savings and enhanced efficiency were also realised, benefiting patients and the healthcare system. Acknowledgements The authors would like to thank all the nurses and doctors in the Preoperative Assessment Clinic (PAC) at Singapore General Hospital, PAC Nurse Clinician NKL, Dr. YZP and the clinic nurses and staff from the Singapore General Hospital Department of Neurology. Finally, the team would like to thank our Quality Improvement Project Coach, SXXS, for her guidance during the project. Footnotes Funding: The authors have not declared a specific grant for this research from any funding agency in the public, commercial or not-for-profit sectors. Patient consent for publication: Not applicable. Ethics approval: According to the policy activities that constitute research at our local tertiary hospital, this work met criteria for operational improvement activities exempt from ethics review. Provenance and peer review: Not commissioned; externally peer reviewed. Patient and public involvement: Patients and/or the public were involved in the design, or conduct, or reporting, or dissemination plans of this research. Refer to the Methods section for further details. Data availability statement Data are available in a public, open access repository. No data are available. References 1. Benesch C, Glance LG, Derdeyn CP, et al. Perioperative Neurological Evaluation and Management to Lower the Risk of Acute Stroke in Patients Undergoing Noncardiac, Nonneurological Surgery: A Scientific Statement From the American Heart Association/American Stroke Association. Circulation. 2021;143:e923–46. doi: 10.1161/CIR.0000000000000968. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Falck-Jones R, Bell M, Hallqvist L. Incidence and risk factors of peri-operative stroke in major non-cardiovascular, non-neurologic surgery-A retrospective register-based cohort study. Acta Anaesthesiol Scand. 2024;68:485–92. doi: 10.1111/aas.14373. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. AbuRahma AF, Avgerinos ED, Chang RW, et al. The Society for Vascular Surgery implementation document for management of extracranial cerebrovascular disease. J Vasc Surg. 2022;75:26S–98S. doi: 10.1016/j.jvs.2021.04.074. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Fanning JP, Campbell BCV, Bulbulia R, et al. Perioperative stroke. Nat Rev Dis Primers. 2024;10:3. doi: 10.1038/s41572-023-00487-6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Mrkobrada M, Chan MTV, Cowan D, et al. Perioperative covert stroke in patients undergoing non-cardiac surgery (NeuroVISION): a prospective cohort study. The Lancet. 2019;394:1022–9. doi: 10.1016/S0140-6736(19)31795-7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement Data are available in a public, open access repository. No data are available.',44,'2025-10-08 11:19:46','2025-10-08 11:19:46');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(597,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10662978','Celebrating 75 Years of Women at the American Academy of Neurology.','Avitzur O, Streeter TS.','2023','trailblazers acceptances iota sorority celebrated mabel masten mabel masten faan mabel masten lick masten masten reese masten masten editorship dejong faan avitzur detienne constable agnosia mistakenly masculinized typo wives faan estelle boynton fae tichy faan faan millikan faan trustees millikan riggs lucy balian rorke myelination riggs riggs orville honored riggs 28th forthright surmount friendship unswerving hears lady millikan constable coauthoring matriculants jumped orly avitzur faan carlayne faan natalia rost faan faha tenures demonstrably coon faan orly avitzur streeter streeter avitzur captimes handwritten masten mabel wisc detienne orly avitzur nyregion tichy pc053 millikan trustees pc053 millikan trustees pc053 gollomp hometown bmctoday practicalneurology pn1018_hth_introducing neuropath neuropath millikan trustees pc053 doshay constable dreams minnpost','Abstract Although the field of neurology was dominated by men at the time the American Academy of Neurology (AAN) was founded in 1948, women were involved from the inception of the organization, albeit in limited numbers. This historical review highlights the challenging experiences of some of those trailblazers who participated in the AAN during the early days and made a mark for themselves in neurology. They helped pave the way for an increasing number of women who now serve in leadership roles—including back-to-back-to-back presidents—and make up 42% of the Academy''s membership today. Only 1 in 5 hospitals accept women for internship, 12 states in the union have no hospitals open to women, in 12 states only 1 hospital accepts them, and in 26 the acceptances vary from 12 to 45 per cent. The situation is not caused by inferior intellectual equipment according to the survey, because records show that the average woman student in medicine has a 10 per cent higher average in her studies than the average male student. So read an article in the Wisconsin Capital Times on June 23, 1940. 1 It was the same year that the Alpha Epsilon Iota Medical Sorority celebrated its 50-year anniversary, with Mabel Garden Masten, MD ( Figure 1 ), the first woman medical intern and resident at Rush Medical College and an associate professor of neuropsychiatry at the University of Wisconsin (UW), as its grand president. The organization was particularly interested in the problem facing women medical students when they graduated: limited opportunities for internship, hospital staff appointments, and academic connections. Figure 1. Mabel G. Masten, MD, FAAN. Open in a new tab Photograph of Mabel Garden Masten, MD, found in the AAN historical archives. Nine years later, when the American Academy of Neurology (AAN) held its inaugural Annual Meeting in 1949 in French Lick, IN, Masten was serving on the Committee on Public Relations, the AAN''s only woman member on a committee. 2 Born on December 29, 1897, in Mineral Point, WI, Masten graduated from the University of Chicago in 1921 and from Rush Medical College with a medical degree in 1925. 3 In a faculty information sheet from the UW completed in her hand on February 23, 1948, she left 1 question blank: “Wife''s maiden name.” After an internship at Wisconsin General Hospital (1925–1926), she did a residency in neuropsychiatry (1926–1927). 3 She later served as chair of the neuropsychiatry department at UW from 1944 to 1945, during then-chair Hans Reese''s absence for military service. 3 Masten resumed her position as chair in 1947 and would hold it in rotation with 3 male colleagues until 1954. 4 Masten became the first woman to join Neurology ® journal''s nascent editorial board in 1950, under the editorship of Russell N. DeJong, MD, FAAN, and became an associate editor in 1954. She also served as vice chair for the American Medical Association''s Nervous and Mental Disease Section Office from 1950 to 1951, according to an email to author Dr. Avitzur from Sara DeTienne, Senior Director of Development in the Department of Neurological Surgery at the University of Wisconsin School of Medicine and Public Health. 5 The AAN invited several women speakers to present talks in 1949''s first Annual Meeting including Kate Constable, MD, who presented a talk titled “Newer drugs in the treatment of parkinsonism,” 6 and Alexandra Adler, MD, who spoke on the “Course and outcome of visual agnosia” 7 —although she was mistakenly listed as “Alexander” Adler in the brochure. 7 Adler, who practiced psychiatry and neurology after coming to this country from Vienna in 1935 to teach and practice at Harvard Medical School and the Massachusetts General Hospital, went on to join the New York University faculty in 1946 and become a full professor of psychiatry at its medical school in 1969. 8 The masculinized typo was not the only indication of assumptions about neurologists. The program brochure also stated, “The registration fee for members is $5 and for their wives $3.” 9 The first membership applications were absent gender selection entirely. 10 Nonetheless, unlike many other professional associations of the time, our Academy accepted women from its inception in 1948, as Sandra F. Olson, MD, FAAN ( Figure 2 ), would point out in her 2005 address, when she became the first woman president of the AAN after 55 years. 11 By the 1950 AAN Annual Meeting, woman members would include Estelle Boynton, MD, from Georgia, and Fae Tichy, MD, PhD, the neuropathologist from the University of Minnesota department of neurology who would become a member of the Committee on Special Courses and “chairman” of the neuropathology course 3 years later. 12 Figure 2. Sandra F. Olson, MD, FAAN. Open in a new tab Photograph of Sandra F. Olson, MD, FAAN, found in the AAN files. A December 30, 1949, letter 13 from the AAN Membership Committee Chair Clark H. Millikan, MD, FAAN, addressed to the Officers and Board of Trustees, reports that Adler was not approved to Fellowship status despite writing “several letters concerning her application for Fellowship and…[being] very anxious that it be given immediate attention.” The reason cited was that the “applicant''s time and interests are devoted largely to Psychiatry.” A year later, a similarly addressed letter from Millikan 14 reported approval for Associate membership of Helena T. Riggs, MD, who later became president of the Philadelphia Neurological Society. 15 She was 1 of 2 people in 1948 to receive the first neuropathology certifications from the American Board of Pathology. 16 As neuropathologist at Philadelphia General Hospital, she and Lucy Balian Rorke-Adams investigated the development of myelin in 107 fetuses over a 5-year period, leading to the publication of Myelination of the Brain in the Newborn in 1969, a year after Riggs'' death. In 1959, Riggs and Orville T. Bailey, MD, started the Diagnostic Slide Sessions at the annual meetings of the American Association of Neuropathologists (AANP). In 2005, the AANP honored the pair with the O.T. Bailey-Helena Riggs Award. Bailey described his colleague at the 28th Annual Session of the AANP on June 13, 1987: She was a diagnostic neuropathologist of great experience and insight, managing the giant neuropathologic material at the Philadelphia General Hospital so that it was a distinguished service. As a member of the AANP, her forthright manner, integrity and common sense helped it to surmount many of its difficult problems in the period when it changed from a small, loosely organized group to the much larger Association it is today. Outside her chosen field, her interests ranged widely and she had a keen understanding of people. Her friendship, once given, was unswerving, and permanent. In short, she exemplified the best of what comes to mind when one hears the expression “Philadelphia Lady.” 17 Two other women approved for AAN Associate membership in a 1952 letter 18 by Millikan were Jean P. Davis, MD, a graduate of the Yale School of Medicine class of 1943, certified by the American Board of Pediatrics and assistant professor of pediatrics at the University of Utah College of Medicine, who at the age of 34 years had 9 publications listed; and the aforementioned Kate Constable, MD, a graduate of Rush Medical College, the University of Chicago in 1925, who was recommended for Associate membership having “written many articles concerning neurology.” This included coauthoring a paper titled “Newer Drugs in the Treatment of Parkinsonism” published in Neurology on January 1, 1951, in its inaugural issue. 19 Over the course of the next 2 decades, women would comprise fewer than 10% of medical school matriculants. With the passage of Title IX of the Higher Education Act in 1972, which prevented federally funded educational institutions from discriminating based on gender, the situation would improve. Within 2 years, the proportion of women entering US medical schools jumped to 22.4%, although the change was not welcome by all, with 1 successful woman physician noting comments she received as a medical student: “How do you think you are going to be a doctor if you have children?” “It won''t affect your grade, but I want you to know that I don''t think women should be doctors,” and “Women just don''t have what it takes to be researchers.” 20 As their numbers grew in the field of neurology, so did women''s involvement in the AAN, with representation on virtually every committee and on the Board of Directors. In 2022, the AAN membership was 42% women, with 113 women serving on committees, representing 46% of participants. Eleven women served on the 24-member Board of Directors. The Neurology journal Editorial Board was 50% women. There have been 3 additional women selected by the Nominations Committee to serve as president since Dr. Olson''s election: Orly Avitzur, MD, MBA, FAAN; Carlayne E. Jackson, MD, FAAN; and Natalia S. Rost, MD, MPH, FAAN, FAHA. Their tenures will span the years 2021–2027. The actions of the AAN over the past several decades demonstrably underscore the organization''s ongoing commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion. Acknowledgment The authors thank Elizabeth A. Coon, MD, FAAN, and the members of the AAN History and Archives Committee for their assistance with research for this article. Glossary AAN American Academy of Neurology AANP American Association of Neuropathologists UW University of Wisconsin Appendix. Authors Name Location Contribution Orly Avitzur, MD, MBA American Academy of Neurology, Minneapolis, MN Drafting/revision of the article for content, including medical writing for content; major role in the acquisition of data Timothy S. Streeter, MA American Academy of Neurology, Minneapolis, MN Drafting/revision of the manuscript for content, including medical writing for content; major role in the acquisition of data Open in a new tab Study Funding No targeted funding reported. Disclosure During writing, T.S. Streeter was employed by the American Academy of Neurology. During writing, O. Avitzur was serving as the President of the American Academy of Neurology. Go to Neurology.org/N for full disclosures. References 1. The Wisconsin Capital Times, Sunday morning edition, June 23, 1940, page 20. Accessed October 26, 2022. captimes.com/ . 2. Program of the First Bi-Annual Meeting of the American Academy of Neurology 1949, p. 6. 3. University of Wisconsin Faculty Information Sheet, handwritten, dated February 23, 1948. University of Wisconsin-Madison Archives Biographical Files collection, File: Masten, Mabel G. 4. University of Wisconsin School of Medicine and Public Health, Department of Neurology. Accessed October 26, 2022, by Kathleen Shannon, MD. neurology.wisc.edu/ . 5. Email from Sara DeTienne to Orly Avitzur. October 26, 2022. 6. Program of the First Bi-Annual Meeting of the American Academy of Neurology 1949, p. 31. 7. Program of the First Bi-Annual Meeting of the American Academy of Neurology 1949, p. 37. 8. Saxon W. New York Times . Dr. Alexandra Adler, 99, Expert on Traumas to Brain. January 12, 2001. Accessed October 12, 2022. nytimes.com/2001/01/12/nyregion/dr-alexandra-adler-99-expert-on-traumas-to-brain.html . 9. Program of the First Bi-Annual Meeting of the American Academy of Neurology 1949, p. 10. 10. Louis ED. Rejected applications: an early American Academy of Neurology struggles to define its membership. Neurology., 2014;83:361. Figure. doi: 10.1212/WNL.0000000000000625 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Olson SF. Women in neurology. Neurology. 2005;65(3):344-348. doi: 10.1212/01.wnl.0000174660.25666.74 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Tichy F. Letter to Neuropathology Course Participants. March 30, 1953. American Academy of Neurology Archives (PC053). Series 2: Annual meeting files. Box 1. 13. Millikan C. Letter to Officers and Board of Trustees of the AAN, December 30, 1949. American Academy of Neurology Archives (PC053). Series 08: Membership Committee Files. 14. Millikan C. Letter to Officers and Board of Trustees of the AAN, March 10, 1950. American Academy of Neurology Archives (PC053). Series 08: Membership Committee Files. 15. Gollomp S. Introducing Hometown Highlights. Practical Neurology . October 2018, p. 18. Accessed December 22, 2022. assets.bmctoday.net/practicalneurology/pdfs/PN1018_HtH_Introducing.pdf . 16. A Brief History of the AANP. Accessed February 2, 2023. neuropath.org/history . [ Google Scholar ] 17. Bailey OT. Origin and early development of the diagnostic slide session. Address to AANP. June 13, 1987. Accessed January 31, 2023. neuropath.org/assets/documents/dss-history-by-bailey.pdf . 18. Millikan C. Letter to Officers and Board of Trustees of the AAN, September 5, 1952. American Academy of Neurology Archives (PC053). Series 08: Membership Committee Files. 19. Doshay LJ, Constable K. Newer drugs in the treatment of Parkinsonism. Neurology. 1951;1(1):68. doi: 10.1212/WNL.1.1.68 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Perry S. How Title IX helped make women''s dreams of becoming doctors a reality. MinnPost . January 23, 2017.',2,'2025-10-08 11:19:59','2025-10-08 11:19:59');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(598,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5957356','Core neurological examination items for neurology clerks: A modified Delphi study with a grass-roots approach.','Liu CH, Hsu LL, Hsiao CT, Hsieh SI, Chang CW, Huang ES, Chang YJ.','2018','beginners gung dejong gung gung 9585b greeting copied instructing instructing cgmh cgmh gung reconsideration goiters bruit kayser fleischer stupor hemi confrontation saccadic nystagmus clenched onion jerk rinne vestibulo uvula shrugging myotomes pronator gower fasciculation pinprick truncal dermatomes triceps brachioradialis patellar patellar babinski bradykinesia tapping chorea ballism athetosis heels romberg hemiplegic dystonic scissors festinating brudzinski kernig wool innervated goiters bruits lacrimation salivation pronator gower tones bradykinesia tapping stupor erection lacrimation salivation pronator stupor thrombolytic siteassets bruit pronator holistically stupor babinski romberg bradykinesia beginners 36kb gung gung gung gung linkou gung cmrpg3g0261 nmrpg3g6411 182a my2 matthias nagasingha ranasinghe gunatilake youssef jozefowicz tubridy ridsdale maghzi minagar clineuro khadilkar bangar chaudhari abushouk duc beeson coburn iobst selden norbury faulk sklar gilhooly mink hofkosh maaz hitzblech holzhausen gunderson kirshner jozefowicz esmaily vahidi amini zarrintan wahlstrom billis arnall oldham kizawa tsuneto tamba takamiya bito rabbani israr memon sr21 crone svirko murto varpio gonsalves chalk 0b013e3181a92be6 rabinstein ackerson adeoye bambakidis aroor mnemonic seet vaucher bovet bengough pidoux grossen panese s12961 schelvis oude hengel wiezer blatter genabeek bohlmeijer boulkedid abdoul loustau sibony alberti e20476 dhaliwal navi pease dhand 0b013e3182315249 aminoff 565e 0f87 chami gavazzi wazieres lejeune carrat piette khodyakov hempel foy schatz replicability 36kb','Abstract Background With the evolution of treatments for neurological diseases, the contents of core neurological examinations (NEs) for medical students may need to be modified. We aimed to establish a consensus on the core NE items for neurology clerks and compare viewpoints between different groups of panelists. Methods First, a pilot group proposed the core contents of NEs for neurology clerks. The proposed core NE items were then subject to a modified web-based Delphi process using the online software “SurveyMonkey”. A total of 30 panelists from different backgrounds (tutors or learners, neurologists or non-neurologists, community hospitals or medical centers, and different academic positions) participated in the modified Delphi process. Each panelist was asked to agree or disagree on the inclusion of each item using a 9-point Likert scale and was encouraged to provide feedback. We also compared viewpoints between different groups of panelists using the Mann-Whitney U test. Results Eighty-three items were used for the first round of the Delphi process. Of them, 18 without consensus of being a core NE item for the neurology clerks in the first round and another 14 items suggested by the panelists were further discussed in the second round. Finally, 75 items with different grades were included in the recommended NE items for neurology clerks. Conclusions Our findings provide a reference regarding the core NE items for milestone development for neurology clerkships. We hope that prioritizing the NE items in this order can help medical students to learn NE more efficiently. Introduction Neurology is regarded as a difficult component of the medical curriculum. Neurophobia is defined as a fear of neurological diseases, and it is a recognized problem among medical students which may prevent them using their basic neurological knowledge at the bedside [ 1 – 6 ]. In addition, this can impede a young doctor’s motivation and confidence of learning neurology and being a neurologist [ 2 , 4 , 7 , 8 ]. Neurophobia may start early in medical school [ 9 ]. Previous studies have suggested effective strategies to cure neurophobia [ 9 ]. Of them, transformation of teaching methods in neurological examinations (NEs) has been shown to play an important role [ 9 ]. Recently, the concept of milestone development has been integrated into medical education [ 10 ]. Different levels of learners should have different entrustable professional activities (EPAs) and corresponding skills in NEs [ 11 – 13 ]. Well trained doctors should be able to efficiently perform task-specific NEs to target the chief complaints and main symptoms, however, performing comprehensive NEs is difficult for beginners. Inadequate preparedness has been associated with stress and anxiety in medical students [ 14 ]. It is reasonable that students should learn about NEs sequentially, starting with the basic but essential items and then advancing to comprehensive knowledge. The essential NE items have previously been established in the core curriculum of neurology clerkship [ 15 ]. With the evolution in epidemiological distribution and treatment of neurological diseases, investigations are needed to determine whether the contents of NE education should be modified in the current era. Using a modified Delphi process [ 16 – 18 ], the aim of this study was to update the consensus on core NE items that should be taught during the NE milestones in neurology clerkships based on the concept of a grass-roots approach [ 19 , 20 ]. Consensus was made by panelists with different backgrounds, and in particular learners and clinical tutors. We also compared viewpoints between different groups of panelists. Materials and methods Definition of neurology clerkship In Taiwan, medical students enter medical school after graduating from senior high school and study there for 6 years. Thereafter, they need to receive 2 years of post-graduate-year training, after which they can apply for residency training. Neurology clerks are 5 th grade medical students who have completed basic medical subjects and lectures on clinical neurology before starting clinical rotation. These students have very little experience in clinical neurology or performing NEs. We assumed that these students would be more likely to experience frustration when encountering the complicated and numerous items of comprehensive NEs. The pilot group We enrolled five attending physicians from the Neurology Department of Chang Gung Memorial Hospital in the pilot group ( Fig 1 ). These participants had different subspecialties in neurology and had actively contributed to medical education for more than 3 years. The objective of the pilot group was to generate a list of proposed core NE items for neurology clerks using NE textbooks (e.g. DeJong’s The Neurologic Examination, 7 th edition) as a reference using a LINE (an online chat application) discussion group. These items were then used in a Delphi process ( S1 and S2 Tables). Fig 1. Flow charts of the pilot group and modified Delphi process. Open in a new tab This figure showing the evolution and decision making process of all of the NE items concluded from the pilot group and each round of the modified Delphi process. NE = neurological examinations. The modified Delphi process The panelists Representativeness and diversity are key factors for panelist selection in the modified Delphi process, and feedback on the elements of training may help to improve this problem [ 21 , 22 ]. The needs of learners and clinical tutors are important in a grass-roots approach [ 19 ]. Therefore, professors, clinical tutors, and learners were the main targets for the panelists in this study [ 23 ]. A total of 30 panelists were enrolled for the modified Delphi process, who were selected from different learning (tutors and learners), expertise (neurologists and non-neurologists), levels of teaching hospital (community hospitals and medical centers), and academic positions. All of the selected learner panelists in this study had qualified academic performance in their neurology class ( Table 1 ). As the neurology clerks may have had limited experience in neurology and therefore may have underestimated or overestimated the true significance of each NE item, we selected the learner panelists from different levels (medical students, interns, and residents). Six general neurologists came from community hospitals. We also enrolled 12 experts from medical centers with different subspecialties, including six from Chang-Gung Memorial Hospital and six from other medical centers. The Ethics Institutional Review Board of Chang Gung Memorial Hospital approved this study (104-9585B), and all of the panelists provided written inform consent. Table 1. Selection criteria of the enrolled panelists. Panelists Selection criteria Clerks Academic performance within the top 50% in their same degree Intern doctors Academic performance within the top 50% in their same degree Residents Clinical performance within the top 50% in their same degree Experts Have >5 years of both educational and clinical experience as an attending physician of the neurological department in medical centers General neurologists Have >3 years of clinical experience as a general neurologist in community hospitals Open in a new tab The survey tool In the traditional Delphi process, meetings should be held with all of the participants. This is time consuming, expensive, and difficult to arrange the attendance of all the clinical physicians. Therefore, in this study, we designed a web-based online anonymous modified Delphi process. We used the online software SurveyMonkey (SM; https://zh.surveymonkey.com/ ) to overcome the problems of distance and time. Personal e-mails were used as the main connection between the principal investigator and the panelists. SM sent e-mails to each panel member with a URL linking to the survey. We first sent a greeting e-mail with educational background surveys before the first round of the modified Delphi process. This step helped to reveal the self-reported expertise, age, and years of neurological education of the panelists. We also sent follow-up reminder e-mails to the non-responders in each round. Each survey round was available for 1 week. We copied the list of responders from each round into new recipient lists for subsequent rounds. The first round In the first round, we requested the panelists to evaluate whether an item should be included or excluded from the core NE items for the neurology clerks. We asked each panelist to agree or disagree with the inclusion of each item using a 9-point Likert scale, from 1 (strongly disagree) to 9 (strongly agree). We discarded the items if less than 10% of the panelists responded to them. We then discussed the items with response rates between 10% and 90% in the next round, and discussed the items with a response rate over 90% in this round. Of them, we defined the items with a score of 9 as strong agreement, and those with scores of 7 and 8 as agreement. The items with the scores between 4 and 6 were also discussed in the next round. We discarded the items with scores between 1 and 3. We asked the 30 panelists to provide feedback, and we also asked them to suggest NE items that were not initially included. We also confirmed the opinions of the panelists by phone to make sure that their suggestions were expressed correctly. The suggested NE items were further evaluated in the next round ( Fig 1 and S1 Table ). The second round We revised the proposed core NE items according to the suggestions and ratings in the first round, and drafted a revised list for the second round. In the second round, we discarded the items if less than 80% of the panelists responded to them, and discussed the items with a response rate of over 80%. Of these items, we discarded the items with scores between 1 and 6. For the items with scores between 7 and 9, those with a response rate less than 90% were also discarded. Following this round, all items were either included or excluded and consensus was achieved ( Fig 1 and S1 Table ). Data collection We recorded the percentage of agreement of the NE items and used the 9-point Likert scale to register the importance of these items in each round of the modified Delphi process. Finally, we generated a recommended core list of NE items for neurology clerks. Statistical analysis All statistical analyses were performed using the Statistical Package for the Social Sciences (IBM SPSS Statistics version 22.0). The Likert scale rating for each NE item from all of the panelists was expressed as median (quartile 1, quartile 3). Items with median scores between 7 and 9 in the first or second round were regarded to be recommended NE items. In addition, we further grouped the panelists based on their backgrounds (tutors and learners, neurologists and non-neurologists, experts in medical center and others) to compare differences in perspective between them using the Mann-Whitney U test (nonparametric data). Statistical significance was set at p < 0.05. Results The proposed core NE items for neurology clerks from the pilot group In total, 98 items were initially provided for open discussion in the pilot group meeting ( S2 Table ). Some of these items were discarded as they were considered to be too difficult for medical students or combined to make the topic more comprehensive. Finally, 83 items were included in the proposed core list of NE items for further discussion in the modified Delphi process ( Fig 1 and S3 Table ). Background characteristics of the panelists Among the panelists, 12 (40%) had experience of performing NEs for 0–10 years, and 18 (60%) had more than 11 years of experience. Fourteen of the panelists (46.7%) had experience of instructing NEs to students for more than 11 years, and 8 (26.7%) had less than 10 years of experience. The panelists included 12 learners (40%) and 18 tutors (60%). Among the 18 tutors, three (16.7%) were professors, four (22.2%) were associate professors, and five (27.8%) were assistant professors. Overall, 33.3% of the tutors were general neurologists in community hospitals. In addition, 10 (55.6%) of the 18 tutors had experience in curriculum design or had served as a program director. The most common expertise of the panelists was general neurology (55.6%), followed by headache (38.9%), cerebrovascular disease (33.3%), and neuro-critical care (33.3%). The panelists in this study covered most fields of subspecialties of clinical neuroscience ( Table 2 ). Table 2. Background characteristics of the panelists. Variables n (%) Time from first performing a NE on patients 0–10 years 12(40.0) 11–20 years 12(40.0) >20 years 6(20.0) Time from first time instructing a NE to students Never 8(26.7) 0–10 years 8(26.7) 11–20 years 8(26.7) >20 years 6(20.0) Position Learners 12 (40%) Year 4 medical students 2(6.7) Clerks 2(6.7) Intern doctors 2(6.7) Post-graduate year 1 residents 2(6.7) Residents (non-neurology) 2(6.7) Residents (neurology) 2(6.7) Tutors 18 (60%) General neurologists 6(20.0) Experts from CGMH 6(20.0) Experts from other medical centers 6(20.0) Subspecialties and academic rank of the tutors Academic rank Professor 3(16.7) Associate professor 4(22.2) Assistant professor 5(27.8) General neurologists 6(33.3) Subspecialties General neurology 10(55.6) Cerebrovascular disease 6(33.3) Cognitive neuroscience and dementia 3(16.7) Epilepsy 3(16.7) Movement disorder 3(16.7) Headache 7(38.9) Neuromuscular disease 5(27.8) Neurological infection 1(5.6) Neuro-critical care 6(33.3) Genetics in neurology 1(5.6) Neurorehabilitation 1(5.6) Sleep medicine 2(11.1) Clinical neurophysiology 3(16.7) Medical education 2(11.1) Open in a new tab CGMH = Chang Gung Memorial Hospital, NE = neurological examination. Results of the first round of the modified Delphi process Twenty-nine (96.7%) of the 30 panelists completed the ratings. All 83 of the core proposed NE items were used for the first round of the modified Delphi process, of which 65 (78.3%) had agreement or strong agreement. Of these 65 items, 16 were strongly recommended as the core competence of neurology clerkship with a rating of 9 ( S4 Table ). However, 18 (21.7%) of the 83 items were put in the second round for further confirmation. Another 14 items were suggested to be added to the core list of NE items by the panelists in the first round ( Fig 1 ). The panelists mentioned the main reasons why these items were considered to be important for neurology clerks in the “comment column” of the online questionnaires ( S5 Table ). Results of the second round of the modified Delphi process The 18 items that were not considered to be core competence for neurology clerks in the first round were discarded after thorough reconsideration in the second round of the modified Delphi process. The ratings of these items were consistent between the two rounds. Ten of the 14 (71.4%) added items suggested by the panelists were regarded to be core competence for neurology clerkship ( S5 Table ). The 22 items discarded during the Delphi process were summarized in the S6 Table . Finally, 75 items were included in the recommended core list of NE items for neurology clerks ( Fig 1 and Table 3 ). Table 3. Results of the modified Delphi process. Categories Rating scores 9 (Strong agreement) 8 (Agreement) 7 (Agreement) Physical examinations 1. Check pulse and heart rate 1. Listen to the heart sounds 2. Check breathing sounds 2. Check thyroid goiters 3. Check neck lymph node examination 4. Listen to carotid bruit 5. Check Kayser-Fleischer rings Conscious and cognitive functions 1. Glasgow coma scale 1. Check complete Mini-Mental State Examination 1. Check language function (reading, writing, repetition, comprehension, fluency, naming) 2. Understanding the definition of coma, semi-coma, stupor, confusion, delirium, and dementia 2. Check hemi-neglect 3. Clock drawing test Cranial nerves 1. Check pupil size and shape 1. Check visual field by confrontation test 1. Check visual acuity by eye chart 2. Check direct li