INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(607,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11699661','Key challenges and countermeasures: a review of undergraduate teaching of neurology in outpatient settings.','Zhu J, Huang J, Cao Y, Cao L.','2025','underappreciated solidifying reformed e1000097 pmed1000097 suspend hone apprehend hmds hmds teleteaching familiarizes conveniently crafting seasoned hone urges perfecting liming jiaqian jingyi liming jingyi yunhan liming yxbjg202412 jiaqian jingyi kalet capponi mahon irby ludmerer flexner nejmra055445 culhane harney mckeague fitzgibbons bordley zinchuk tubridy yazdi feyzabadi gozashti komsari mohammadtaghizadeh amiresmaili kerman biesalski kirchbauer doc69 zma001362 s12939 ramani leinster wiel boshuizen scherpbier illing nee kergon burford baldauf burford whittle vance informatization jcer v7i4 eimss atlantis thistlethwaite ekeocha macdougall e421 moorthy mededpublish hew aschenbrener raghavendra arja e12553 jjns barteit lanfermann barnighausen neuhann beiersmann e29080 kononowicz woodham edelbring stathakarou e14676 venail deveze lallemant guevara mondain e282 waseh dicker e12515 mian dicapua leydon loomis longbrake vased jitt nuamah adapa mazur mccutcheon lohan traynor briscoe peglow e0273963 0b013e31828766ff jilin issn1673 centeno dolmans gelula nutter coller alessandri gewertz nora mavis ogle smee dornan muijtjens scherpbier boshuizen mcpi shrivastava bederson quantitating jns203900 norcini mou jaoa keeley rebella frohna','Abstract In the modern medical education system, teaching of clinical neurology in outpatient settings is crucial for training future neurologists. The neurology outpatient clinic is a pivotal setting for both initial consultations and follow-up visits. It plays a significant role in the prevention, diagnosis, treatment, and ongoing monitoring of neurological disorders, and is a critical platform for clinical education. Under the guidance of experienced physicians during their clinical rotations, medical students enhance their clinical reasoning ability and skills by learning about the diagnosis and treatment of neurological diseases, thereby laying a solid foundation for becoming competent doctors. Despite the advancements in this field, there is a lack of comprehensive reports on the current status and challenges of teaching neurology in outpatient settings. This gap significantly impedes the development of scientific policies to improve teaching standards. We need to clarify the existing issues to develop effective strategies, such as actively embracing advanced educational achievements and experiences, continuously refining teaching models and methods, and enhancing the quality of education, to cultivate more outstanding medical professionals. This article summarizes the current state and issues of teaching clinical neurology in outpatient settings, and analyzes countermeasures to provide a foundation for future practice and study. Keywords: Neurology, Outpatient setting, Teaching, Current situation, Countermeasures Background As the aging population increases, the incidence of neurological diseases also increases annually. making neurology an increasingly important branch of clinical medicine [ 1 ]. With the growing number of patients with neurological disorders treated at outpatient clinics, these settings have become vital educational environments for medical students [ 2 , 3 ], playing an active role in their training [ 4 ]. Clinical rotations serve as a crucial bridge between theory and practice, facilitating students’ transition into professional physicians. During their rotation in neurology outpatient clinics, students directly interact with patients, observe disease manifestations, learn how to collect medical histories, and conduct physical examinations [ 5 ]. The Council on Undergraduate Medical Education in the United States has emphasized the need to strengthen the clinical training of medical students in outpatient settings [ 6 ]. Neurological disorders are complex, and medical students, both in China and other countries, generally find mastering neurology challenging [ 3 , 7 ]. The main reasons for these difficulties include the complexity of neuroanatomy, limited patient exposure, and insufficient teaching [ 7 ]. In neurology outpatient clinics, medical students have the opportunity to interact with a large number of patients, including those who are often difficult to encounter in wards. This is instrumental in broadening their horizons and accumulating experience and skills, which is why outpatient clinical teaching is crucial. Nevertheless, the importance of outpatient neurology rotations is still underappreciated in many places, with teaching methods and models being overly simplistic. In modern medical education, clinical rotations are an essential teaching model that is vital for the development of clinical skills and professional conduct in medical students. Neurology outpatient clinics, where patients receive initial consultations and follow-ups, play a crucial role in the prevention, diagnosis, treatment, and monitoring of neurological diseases [ 1 ]. They provide indispensable platforms for clinical teaching, and complement ward-based education [ 2 ]. Medical students have the opportunity to interact directly with patients, learning and mastering diagnostic and treatment skills for neurological conditions. Under the guidance of their clinical instructors, students apply clinical reasoning to perform diagnostic assessments and develop and implement treatment plans, thereby solidifying their theoretical knowledge and skills and enhancing their clinical management capabilities. With the advancements in technology and methods, neurology outpatient teaching must be continuously explored and reformed to cultivate exceptional clinicians. This article analyzes the current state of outpatient clinical teaching of neurology, proposes strategies for improvement, and reviews the latest developments in this field. Methods Search strategy A literature search was conducted using the PubMed database for articles published in English and Chinese up to March 1, 2024. The initial search strategy for publications was performed by two authors using the following keywords: “undergraduate teaching” OR “university teaching” OR “undergraduate medical education” AND “neurology” AND “outpatient settings.” No restrictions, limits, or filters were applied. Eligibility criteria Original research articles and literature reviews were included in the study. Only literature that aligned with the research topic of “Undergraduate Teaching of Neurology in Outpatient Settings” was considered. Titles and abstracts were screened by two independent reviewers, and full-text publications were evaluated to further include or exclude studies. Duplicate references and redundant publications were excluded. The reviewers also conducted checks of the citation lists in the included articles for additional references. Any disagreements were resolved through negotiation or by consulting with the corresponding author. Results Of the 129 studies identified, 19 met the inclusion criteria, with full-text articles on undergraduate teaching of neurology available in both English and Chinese, and were included in the final analysis. Figure 1 presents the PRISMA flow diagram. Based on the final included literature, we have summarized “The Key Challenges of Internship Teaching in Clinical Neurology” as follows: Fig. 1. Open in a new tab PRISMA 2009 flow diagram. From: Moher D, Liberati A, Tetzlaff J, Altman DG, The PRISMA Group (2009). Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses: The PRISMA Statement. PLoS Med 6(6): e1000097. doi:10.1371/journal.pmed1000097. For more information, visit www.prisma-statement.org 1) The evaluation system for outpatient teaching of neurology is not yet comprehensive enough. 2) The methods used in outpatient teaching of clinical neurology need to be updated. 3) There is an uneven distribution and level of medical teaching resources. 4) The clinical experience offered to the medical student is insufficient. Discussion The evaluation system for outpatient teaching of neurology is not yet comprehensive enough Common shortcomings of the evaluation system for outpatient teaching of clinical neurology include the following: (1) Lack of objective criteria: There is an absence of clear, objective standards to gauge various facets of outpatient teaching of clinical neurology, including diagnostic proficiency, treatment planning, and communication skills. (2) Limited feedback mechanisms: Feedback is inadequately collected from both learners and patients, which hinders comprehensive assessment of the teaching methodology. (3) Emphasis on traditional metrics: Evaluations excessively emphasize conventional metrics such as attendance, case presentations, and physical examination skills, while neglecting other vital elements such as communication skills, clinical reasoning, and decision-making. The methods used in outpatient teaching of clinical neurology need to be updated Traditional outpatient neurology rotations for medical students prioritize observation over hands-on experience, as observational teaching is easier to implement. The clinical services provided by teaching hospitals in a study conducted in Iran did not meet the students’ expectations, and there were significant differences between the expectations of the quality of services among the medical students [ 8 ]. The traditional ward-centric approaches to teaching may not be ideal for the evolving landscape of outpatient neurology education. The coronavirus disease emerged in China in late 2019, forcing the Chinese government to suspend classes and clinical rotations. A study on remote clinical training practices during the coronavirus disease pandemic highlighted the need for innovative approaches to neurology rotations, emphasizing the importance of distance education and virtual clinical training [ 9 ]. In the future, on-site rotations should be combined with virtual clinical training to fully utilize their advantages, including increased flexibility, accessibility, and a broader range of learning resources, thereby improving the overall efficiency of clinical rotation programs. There is an uneven distribution and level of medical teaching resources Several factors contribute to this issue: (1) Geographic disparities: Medical teaching resources may be concentrated in urban areas, leading to a lack of teaching resources in rural or underserved regions [ 10 ]. This disparity limits opportunities for medical students in these areas to receive high-quality education and training. (2) Variability in infrastructure: Some medical institutions possess state-of-the-art facilities, advanced technology, and well-equipped teaching hospitals, whereas others may lack these resources. This disparity results in significant differences in the quality of education and clinical training across institutions. (3) Faculty expertise: The availability of rigorous and knowledgeable faculty members can vary across medical institutions, affecting the depth and quality of medical education provided to students. In some regions, there is a scarcity of clinical neurology instructors, which results in an inconsistent quality of teaching provided during clinical rotations. This makes it difficult to ensure standardized teaching [ 11 ]. (4) Some hospitals do not prioritize teaching, and the immense work pressure and heavy research responsibilities often limit instructors’ time and energy, preventing them from providing adequate teaching services [ 12 ]. Additionally, some physicians may lack systematic training in teaching methods and techniques, making them unable to deliver high-quality teaching. The clinical experience offered to the medical student is insufficient There are multiple reasons for this insufficiency: (1) Limited training time: With increasing specialization across medical disciplines, the time allotted for each specialty rotation is limited, potentially hindering students from achieving satisfactory learning outcomes. (2) Lack of practical operation: Undergraduate training is relatively adequate in terms of theoretical learning and physical examination skills, but it falls short in practical matters, patient management skills, and teamwork [ 13 ]. Medical students report limited learning gains from clinical practice, especially regarding ward work, managing acute clinical situations, writing prescriptions, prioritizing clinical tasks, managing time, and dealing with paperwork [ 14 ]. Many graduates ( n = 344) from UK medical schools reported having few “hands-on” experiences in providing immediate care during their final year placements [ 15 ]. (3) Inadequate supervision: The lack of experienced mentors or attending physicians to guide and supervise students can hinder the acquisition of clinical skills and experience. Despite the significant challenges outlined above in terms of undergraduate neurology education in outpatient settings, we have identified the following key strategies: Improving the supervision of clinical rotation teaching quality A teaching quality monitoring system suitable for clinical practice teaching is imperative [ 16 ]. Such a system should be implemented to assess teaching quality from the perspectives of information and operations. It should specifically include objectives and decision-making related to teaching quality, as well as information, evaluation, assurance, incentive, and feedback mechanisms [ 17 ]. In addition, researchers have established an “Internet +” teaching quality evaluation and monitoring system with the characteristics of teaching hospitals affiliated to local medical colleges to standardize the training system of clinical teachers and achieve self-monitoring and improvement in terms of teaching quality [ 16 ]. Utilizing advanced teaching methods The main strategies are as follows: (1) Case-based learning: Case-based learning helps students connect theoretical knowledge with clinical practice through the use of authentic cases. This approach promotes a deeper understanding and enhanced application of knowledge by enabling students to apply their knowledge to real clinical scenarios [ 18 ]. (2) Simulation-based training: Simulation-based training uses simulators or other technologies to replicate real-world environments, providing students with a safe and controlled space to hone and improve their clinical skills [ 19 ]. Simulation-based training was more effective than WeChat-assisted teaching and demonstration in promoting clinical skills, academic interest, compassion, and empathy in students [ 20 ]. (3) Diversified teaching methods: These methods include flipped classrooms [ 21 ], problem-based learning [ 22 ], evidence-based medicine (EBM) [ 23 ], and “shadow teaching” [ 24 ], which should be fully utilized to enhance student interest and engagement. The flipped classroom is a promising teaching approach for increasing learner motivation and engagement [ 25 ]. EBM is an integrative and comprehensive method to successfully adopt the concept of acquired medical knowledge for effective medical practice. Early introduction of EBM in the preclinical years has proven favorable to students, better equipping them with the ability to critically apprehend and appraise new research and innovations in medicine [ 23 ]. (4) Virtual and augmented reality enhancements [ 26 ]: Virtual reality (VR) and augmented reality (AR) applications simulate neurological examinations, diagnostic procedures, and patient encounters, providing learners with immersive and interactive learning experiences. Training with VR- and AR-based head-mounted devices (HMDs) is salient, motivating, and engaging. Compared with traditional education, virtual clinical education can more effectively improve skills and at least as effectively improve knowledge [ 27 ]. Employing 3D imaging software allows students to delve into intricate neuroanatomy, offering an enhanced comprehension of neurological structures and diseases [ 28 ]. Extended reality-based HMDs offer advantages in medical education, yielding results that are at least as effective as traditional teaching and training methods [ 26 ]. (5) Telemedicine platforms: Telemedicine platforms enable learners to participate in virtual patient consultations, observe remote neurological assessments, and gain exposure to telehealth technologies in neurological outpatient care [ 27 ]. Interactions with telemedicine technologies during undergraduate medical training contribute to the improvement of core competencies, medical knowledge, and overall learning [ 29 ]. Live teleteaching videoconference platforms preserve student engagement and interactivity [ 30 ]. Using video-based lectures has proven to be a feasible method for enhancing learning efficiency during a clinical clerkship in neurology [ 31 ]. (6) Electronic health records training [ 32 ]: Providing hands-on training using electronic health record systems familiarizes learners with documentation practices, patient data management, and the utilization of digital health records in outpatient neurology settings. Combining on-site clinical rotations with virtual clinical rotations can fully utilize their advantages and maximize efficiency. (7) Online learning platforms [ 33 ]: Using online learning platforms to deliver interactive neurology modules, virtual patient case studies, and self-assessment tools enables learners to conveniently access educational content and engage in self-directed learning [ 27 ]. Available evidence suggests that online learning for teaching clinical skills is no less effective than traditional means [ 24 ]. Some medical schools have transitioned from traditional in-person lecture-based teaching to alternative methods, such as online teaching, to facilitate student learning [ 34 ]. Guidelines and standards should be formulated to enhance the efficiency of virtual clinical rotations, and effective measures should be taken to build better online learning platforms [ 35 ]. Enhancing the construction of teaching staff Building a stable teaching faculty team in outpatient clinics is challenging because of tight outpatient schedules and difficulties in controlling the workload. It requires experienced and high-quality teaching staff who can ensure the quality of education and medical care [ 11 ]. Therefore, we recommend adopting the following strategies: (1) Faculty development programs: These programs aim to enhance teaching skills, clinical supervision, feedback delivery, and the integration of modern educational methods into outpatient teaching of clinical neurology. Neurology faculty members should be encouraged to participate in interdisciplinary training and collaborate with educators from other healthcare disciplines to explore innovative teaching approaches and interdisciplinary education [ 36 ]. Programs should be established to support junior faculty members in their professional development, fostering a culture of continuous learning and growth among the teaching staff. (2) Integration of clinical educators: Having practicing neurologists and outpatient specialists as part of the teaching team will provide real-world clinical insights and enhance the translation of clinical expertise into effective teaching practices. (3) Collaboration with educational specialists: Collaboration between the neurology faculty members and educational specialists, such as instructional designers and curriculum developers, should be promoted to ensure the effective design and delivery of educational content for outpatient teaching of clinical neurology. (4) Recognition of teaching excellence: Recognition of excellence in medical teaching should be a holistic process that is based on several criteria: teaching effectiveness, engagement in educational scholarships, contributions to curriculum development, and the ability to foster a positive learning environment. Mechanisms should be implemented to recognize and reward teaching excellence within the neurology faculty and promote a culture of appreciation for educators who demonstrate exceptional teaching skills [ 37 , 38 ]. (5) Scholarly activities in education: Neurology staff should be encouraged to participate in scholarly activities related to medical education, such as conducting educational research, publishing pedagogical innovations, and presenting at educational conferences. Workshops and seminars can positively impact the knowledge, attitudes, skills, and working institutions of medical educators [ 39 ]. (6) Training in assessment and evaluation: Faculty members should be trained in crafting comprehensive assessment tools, implementing effective evaluation strategies, and utilizing technology for educational assessment in clinical neurology outpatient settings. Principle areas for training assessment include key competencies, learning outcomes, skills development, and a range of assessment techniques, including surveys, quizzes, practical demonstrations, peer evaluations, and self-assessments. Educational activities should be objectively measured by building a relative value unit system [ 40 ]. A feedback system should be created to allow faculty members to gather insights from learners, colleagues, and supervisory personnel, thereby facilitating the ongoing enhancement of teaching methods and educational outcomes. Prioritizing the cultivation of students’ clinical skills The following strategies are relevant: (1) Early and more frequent patient exposure: This ensures that students have early and frequent interactions with neurology patients to build confidence in managing neurological conditions. Well-executed bedside teaching can provide lasting lessons for learners [ 41 ]. Over 80% of the respondents felt that neurology teaching could be improved through greater exposure to patients [ 7 ]. (2) Structured observations: Creating structured opportunities for students allows them to observe experienced neurologists perform patient assessments, neurological examinations, and diagnostic procedures, facilitating learning. (3) Preceptorship and mentorship: Connecting students with seasoned neurology preceptors and mentors for guidance, feedback, and role modeling enhances the development of clinical skills. (4) Standardized patient encounters: Utilizing standardized patient simulations enables students to hone history-taking, physical examination, and communication skills in a controlled setting [ 42 ]. (5) Skill-based assessments: Implementing skill-based assessments, such as the objective structured clinical examination method of skills assessment, will enable students to understand their grasp of clinical competency [ 43 ]. (6) Clinical reasoning training: Embedding training in clinical reasoning and differential diagnosis emphasizes a systematic approach to neurological case assessment and diagnostic decision-making. The promotion of reflective practice urges students to critically evaluate their clinical experiences. Outlook First, the quality management system for clinical teaching needs to be continuously improved. Neurology outpatient clinical teaching places more emphasis on the evaluation of teaching quality and effectiveness, such that schools and hospitals can more accurately grasp the students’ clinical ability and intervene in a timely manner. Various tools [ 11 , 44 ] and methods [ 13 , 45 ] for assessing teaching quality have been developed and urgently require peer learning and active application. The American Board of Internal Medicine promotes the use of direct observation methods to assess the clinical skills of post-graduate doctors. For example, the Mini-Clinical Evaluation Exercise was developed to focus on outpatient cases [ 46 ]. Although many tools are available for the direct observation of clinical skills, there is a scarcity of valid evidence and detailed descriptions of educational outcomes [ 47 ]. Some hospitals have established sound teaching systems, including selection, training, assessment, and incentive mechanisms for teaching physicians [ 48 ]. These systems should undergo ongoing refinement and validation. Second, there have been constant innovations in teaching methods and models. The instructional approach to neurology outpatient rotations has evolved from traditional face-to-face teaching to a combination of modern educational technologies and methods, including multimedia [ 49 ], online courses, standardized patients, virtual patient simulations, and validated standardized checklists [ 50 ]. This diversification of teaching formats aims to stimulate interest in learning and improve clinical skills and decision-making abilities. Updating and integrating the teaching model for outpatient clinical rotations in neurology should include the Problem-Based Medical Education model and incorporate student-centered educational approaches. Additionally, the development of students’ clinical skills should continuously be prioritized. The integration of basic neuroscience and clinical neurology in outpatient teaching practices should be emphasized, while enhancing students’ innovation abilities. Conclusions Despite the significant progress made in terms of teaching clinical neurology, several challenges must be addressed. This is particularly evident in the fact that the current evaluation system for outpatient teaching in this field is insufficient. Additionally, the teaching methods related to outpatient clinical neurology practice require updating. The medical teaching resources, as well as the clinical experience of medical student, is often lacking. Research concerning clinical neurology education should continuously explore and innovate, and constantly improve and enhance. This includes optimizing teaching models, perfecting the evaluation system, and making rational use of modern educational methods to effectively improve the quality of clinical teaching and cultivate outstanding clinical physicians. Acknowledgements We would like to thank the “Double-First Class” Application Characteristic Discipline of Hunan Province (Pharmaceutical Science) for the support. Abbreviations AR Augmented reality EBM Evidence-based medicine EMR Electronic medical record HMD Head-mounted device VR Virtual reality Author contributions Conceptualization and Resources: Liming Cao. Writing—Original Draft Preparation: Jiaqian Zhu, Jingyi Huang, and Liming Cao. Jingyi Huang, and Yunhan Cao provided constructive discussion. Writing—Review & Editing: Liming Cao. Funding This study received funding from the Educational and Teaching Reform Research Project of the First Affiliated Hospital of Shenzhen University (202209), Medical Education Reform Research Project of the Medical School of Shenzhen University (YXBJG202412), and Shenzhen Second People’s Hospital Clinical Research Fund of the Shenzhen High-level Hospital Construction Project (20243357001). Data availability Data sharing is not applicable to this article as no datasets were generated or analyzed during the current study. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate Not applicable. Consent for publication Not applicable. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. Jiaqian Zhu and Jingyi Huang contributed equally to this work and share the first authorship. References 1. GBD 2016 Neurology Collaborators. Global, regional, and national burden of neurological disorders, 1990–2016: a systematic analysis for the global burden of Disease Study 2016. Lancet Neurol. 2019;18(5):459–80. 10.1016/S1474-4422(18)30499-X. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Carvalho V, Moro E. Setting and outcomes in neurology teaching: where shou