 knowledge during the Cambrian explosion. Behav Brain Sci. 2021;44: e174. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Walsh K. The Cost Bubble in Medical Education: Will it Burst and When? Ann Med Health Sci Res. 2016;6(4):257–9. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Maxwell WD, Fabel PH, Diaz V, Walkow JC, Kwiek NC, Kanchanaraksa S, et al. Massive open online courses in U.S. healthcare education: Practical considerations and lessons learned from implementation. Curr Pharm Teach Learn. 2018;10(6):736–43. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Hendriks RA, de Jong PGM, Admiraal WF, Reinders MEJ. Instructional design quality in medical Massive Open Online Courses for integration into campus education. Med Teach. 2020;42(2):156–63. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Mahajan R, Gupta P, Singh T. Massive Open Online Courses: Concept and Implications. Indian Pediatr. 2019;56(6):489–95. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Vaysse C, Chantalat E, Beyne-Rauzy O, Morineau L, Despas F, Bachaud JM, et al. The impact of a small private online course as a new approach to teaching oncology: development and evaluation. JMIR Med Educ. 2018;4(1):e6. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 28. Arantes M, Arantes J, Ferreira MA. Tools and resources for neuroanatomy education: a systematic review. BMC Med Educ. 2018;18(1):94. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 29. Venter G, Lubbe JC, Bosman MC. Neurophobia: A Side Effect of Neuroanatomy Education? J Med Syst. 2022;46(12):99. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.',12,'2025-10-08 11:29:44','2025-10-08 11:29:44');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(645,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11338498','Analysis of Gender Discrepancies in Leadership Roles and Recognition Awards in the Child Neurology Society.','Martindale JM, Christy AL, Gombolay GY, Aravamuthan BR, Jansen L, Joshi S, Strober JB, Terrell M, Tilton AH, Pearl PL, Silver JK, Mink JW, Khakoo Y.','2024','nominee nonpresidential sexism rationalize homemakers benevolence agentic decisiveness congruity likability agentic backlash agentic derogatory furthering ladder rung sticky ladder sabotaging matilda miscredited candidacy nominating nominees nominees ballots hower brumback martha denckla nominees templated rubic nominating sidney pronouns obituary irb00098283 bods treasurers councilors treasurer councilor treasurer councilor nonpresidential nominating nominees nonpresidential monique terrell plateaued brumback hower hower martha denckla brumback hower yia yia nominees nominees martha denckla brumback hower yia treasurer councilor noncouncilor councilor yia nominees elections noncouncilor nominees nominees treasurer nominees councilor nominees humbling rationalize nominating brumback brumback hower nominees nonbiased favoritism nominating nominees nominating noncouncilor nominator nominate likability ldei bylaws nominee nominating candidacy hedging raisers grindstone adjectives nominating candidacy nominate ldei nominee identically paywalls sidney distinguishment bylaws sexism bylaw nonpresident ldei strategize ageism transparently ldei obtainable nominees nominator nominee nominee sexism sidney ldei ldei jaclyn martindale christy providence gombolay bhooma aravamuthan dphil charlottesville sucheta strober monique terrell tilton lsu phillip spaulding mink pittsford yasmin khakoo e209838 ul1tr002378 kl2tr002381 martindale christy councillor biohaven gombolay flaccid aravamuthan neurocrine councillor stoke epigenix strober biohaven alexion terrell tilton treasurer councillor hower medlink councillor nominating hower royalty medlink demos binational venture mink councillor nominating hower theranexus neurogene dsmbs emalex royalty khakoo councillor omslife kirch hingle sticky e2010985 wijnen massen kret schor decanal decanal tiwana sticky ceilings e24080 slocum ghalib poorman eagly maceachran erlbaum eagly eagly karau congruity 295x tiedens backlash bul0000039 rudman feminized backlash agentic gentler agentic individ rudman backlash agentic cannady problematizing sce ditch ditch vela nakae e2321926 hermsen ovadia vanneman sof sirawsky ladder rung womensleadershiptoday ladder rung berheide sticky rossiter matilda merillat weathers ney e893 e902 formanowicz spb hofstra najar galvez jurafsky swaiman carnes handelsman marcotte ganguli promotions thorndyke professorships ryn carnes stereotype 0b013e318248f718 carnes hitting blauwet e1003903 polsky ashwal mintz tajfel rudman counterstereotypical trix psenka madera hebl valian s10869 steinpreis ritzke hassett e465 e472 tesch helwig nattinger sticky halley rustagi k4926 k4926 adesoye mangurian girgis sabry elnaggar linos moms brumback augustine mink denounce bods','Abstract Background and Objectives Gender disparities have been demonstrated across several medical specialties, including neurology. Although women have comprised most of the child neurology trainees since 2007, it is not apparent whether this demographic shift is reflected in the Child Neurology Society (CNS) awards and leadership. This study aimed to evaluate the differences in gender representation among leadership positions and award recipients within the CNS. The primary outcome measure was the total number of board of director (BOD) positions or awards given by gender each year. Methods A retrospective review of publicly available data was conducted on CNS members, post-training award recipients, and BOD positions, including nomination records, from 1972 to 2023. Data abstracted were restricted to gender to preserve member and nominee anonymity. Gender identification and consensus were determined through a combination of strategies and study members. Data analysis included descriptive statistics, Pearson χ 2 test, and the exact binomial test to compare gender proportions and the probability of being underrepresented in awards, leadership, and nominations over time. Data are presented according to the Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology guidelines. Results From 1972 to 2023, women represented 29% (44/152) of the BOD positions and 26% (61/236) of post-training award recipients presented by the CNS. Despite the increase in the proportion of women in child neurology, the overall gap in gender representation in leadership positions remains broadly stable. Only 13% (4/32) of CNS presidents have been women, a significant underrepresentation (95% CI 2.3%–52%, p < 0.004), although the representation of women in nonpresidential positions increased from 2003 to 2023. Women are also underrepresented as overall awardees (95% CI 12%–38%, p < 0.00001) except for the Philip R. Dodge Young Investigator Award, which is an investigator-initiated application. Discussion Women remain underrepresented at the highest levels of recognition in child neurology despite representing most of the field. Reasons for disparities are known to be multifactorial and likely include gender bias and structural sexism. We present several discussion topics that seek to rationalize this disparity and provide suggestions for improving diversity, equity, and inclusion for leadership roles and awards. Introduction The representation of women in medicine has increased, and for the first time, women accounted for more than half (50.7%) of incoming US medical students in 2017. 1 However, studies show that gender equity, particularly in professional recognition and leadership positions, has not matched the same progress. Gender disparities start early in medical training and broaden with career progression. 2 , 3 Across medicine, women are underrepresented in leadership and recognition in medical schools, 4 department chairs, 5 and medical and surgical specialty societies. 6 , 7 Moreover, women are less likely to be promoted than men even when controlling for external family commitments, years of service, and research productivity. 5 , 8 Several theories have been used to explain gender bias. Social role theory was based on gender stereotypes and expected gender roles in society. 9 Women were seen as traditional homemakers and caretakers, compared with men, who were assumed to be decision makers and heads of households. Despite changing gender roles, gender role identity theory suggests that gender stereotypes continue to be taught at a young age. 10 Women are expected to display communal traits (benevolence, trustworthiness, nurturing, caring) whereas stereotypical men are expected to display agentic traits (assertive, confident, competent, decisiveness). According to role congruity theory, individuals are viewed less favorably when they do not align with their expected traits. 11 Women may be evaluated differently than men in likability 12 and competence. 13 While successful leaders tend to have agentic traits, 14 backlash and prejudice are common against agentic women in leadership positions. 15 The leaky pipeline theory suggests that women leave or “leak” out of academic medicine during career progression. This theory has been largely criticized because it assumes that women are “opting out” of 1 linear trajectory in academia. It fails to account for alternative career paths and the influence of organizational disparities. 16 , 17 Furthermore, given concerns about environmental racism, there has been a call to remove the pipeline metaphor from medicine in favor of a less derogatory term, “pathway.” 18 The glass ceiling effect refers to women''s invisible barriers to furthering their career advancement (getting to the highest positions in medicine or winning top awards). 19 Alternatively, there is the broken ladder or broken rung effect caused by conscious or unconscious biases faced from the beginning of a woman''s career. 20 The sticky floor effect refers to obstacles faced, even getting on the ladder and being “stuck” at the lowest entry level of academia. 21 Some describe this as a product of self-sabotaging while others describe this as secondary to inequities. Finally, the Matilda effect describes how women receive less credit for their scholarship than men or even have their work miscredited to men. 22 Neurology is not exempt from gender bias and disparities. Only 26% (401/1,541) of full professors in neurology are women. 23 In addition, neurology has one of the most significant gender pay gaps for any medical speciality. 24 There are documented gender disparities across all facets of academia, each playing a pivotal role in career advancement and, more broadly, leading to lost opportunities. Representation bias eventually affects the ability of people to meet and collaborate, decreasing scientific quality, innovation, and progress for all. 25 , 26 The Child Neurology Society (CNS) was founded in 1972 to support child neurologists. The CNS included 15.2% (22/144) of women at the first annual CNS meeting in 1972 and 223 members once officially formed. 27 Although representation of women is increasing in child neurology training programs, with women comprising most of the child neurology trainees since 2007, according to the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME), 28 it is not apparent whether this demographic shift is reflected in CNS awards and leadership positions. Additional ACGME data are available in eFigure 1. This study aimed to evaluate the differences in gender representation among leadership positions and award recipients within the CNS. This work is essential to hold organizations accountable for the equity and inclusion necessary for optimal scientific and medical progress. Leadership Positions There are 3 types of board of director (BOD) positions within the CNS, which have a peer or self-nomination process. Additional descriptions are available in eTable 1. Nominations require a candidate''s curriculum vitae (CV), a candidacy statement, and, starting in 2020, a diversity statement. Nominating committee members, chosen by the president, review all nominations, select those who meet the CNS service and qualification threshold, and then rank the nominees. The committee meets to review scores, discuss, and agree on a final ranking of candidates. The top 2 candidates become the election nominees and submit statements describing their service, experience, commitment to diversity, and goals for the CNS. Electronic ballots are emailed to all members for voting. Awards The CNS has 8 major faculty-level awards. Additional descriptions are available in eTable 2. 6 of these 8 awards (Hower, Bernard Sachs, Philip R. Dodge Young Investigator, Roger and Mary Brumback Lifetime Achievement, Arnold P. Gold Foundation Humanism in Medicine, and Martha Bridge Denckla) are selected by the CNS Awards Committee, whose members are appointed by the CNS president. 5 of these 6 awards, except for the Philip R. Dodge Young Investigator Award (Dodge), have a peer nomination process. Although self-nominations are allowed for these awards, self-nominations are rare. The Awards Committee selects awardees for these 5 awards based on their nomination package and personal experience or impressions of the nominees. Since 2022, the Dodge Award uses a modified templated rubic to score the applicant''s research grant proposal. The CNS International Affairs Committee selects the Bernard D''Souza International Fellowship Award (D''Souza). The Training Director Award is chosen by an ad hoc CNS joint selection group comprising 2 CNS members and 3 Professors and Educators of Child Neurology members. In addition, several trainee-level awards are not included in our data. Methods Data Collection A retrospective review of publicly available data was performed for all awardees and a list of those who held office in the CNS from 1972 to 2023. Membership data, anonymous aggregated gendered nomination records for awards, and anonymous aggregated gendered nomination records for board positions were obtained in the following ways: (1) awards committee current and past chairs, (2) nominating committee current and past chairs, and (3) the CNS executive director. American Academy of Neurology (AAN) Membership Data Services provided a list of Sidney Carter Child Neurology Award recipients. Data on current and past presidents from the AAN and American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) were publicly available. Gender, not sex, was used in this study. It is important to note that, although gender is fluid and nonbinary, we used binary groups (i.e., men or women) for this study. Although this is an incomplete representation of gender, it allowed the best approximation of the representation of women in CNS awards and leadership positions for this study. Within the CNS membership, gender choices are limited and optional. Gender was identified through a combination of pronouns, biographies, photographs, and first names in the following ways: (1) CNS website, (2) obituary announcements for those who had passed away, (3) Wikipedia article, (4) census records, (5) public profile or institutional website, and then (6) newspaper articles. For first names that were not associated with a woman or man, Gender API was used. A consensus on gender was determined by multiple team members (J.M.M., A.C., G.G., B.A.). Discussion topics and mitigation strategies were identified through group email discussion between several team members (J.M.M., A.C., G.G., B.A., Y.K., J.W.M.), incorporating previous reviewer''s feedback, and review of the literature. 6 , 25 , 29 - 36 Statistical Analysis The primary outcome measures were the total number of awards or BOD positions given to women and men each year over each assessed CNS award or BOD position. We also compared gender proportions by award, nomination process, and BOD position to see whether the percentages changed over time. We calculated the gender disparity of BOD positions and awards over time. We performed 2 types of statistical analyses to assess whether the proportion of women who won awards or held board positions was comparable with that of women in the CNS membership. First, we used the Pearson χ 2 test to compare the proportions of women who won awards or held board positions with those of women in the CNS until that year. Then, we used the exact binomial test to compare the probability of being underrepresented. The proportions for comparison were used from 2007 to 2021 because these data were available from the ACGME. Statistical analyses were performed using SAS 16.0 (Cary, NC) and Excel. Standard Protocol Approvals, Registrations, and Patient Consents This study was deemed exempt from Wake Forest University Health Sciences Institutional Review Board review (IRB00098283). Consent was not obtained. Data are presented according to the Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology guidelines. 37 Data Availability Data on past BODs and award recipients of the CNS are publicly available. In addition, compiled deidentified data within this article may be made available by request to the first author by a qualified investigator. Results Overall In the 51 years reviewed (1972–2023), the CNS presented 152 BOD positions and 236 recognition awards. Our primary focus was on gender representation among CNS leadership and awardees. Overall, women represented 29% (44/152) of the BOD positions and 26% (61/236) of the award recipients, with the same women being recognized multiple times. For example, 81 unique women accounted for the 106 leadership positions and recognition awards held by women in the CNS between 1972 and 2023. Excluding the D''Souza Award, 62 women accounted for the remaining 71 positions and awards. The proportions of women child neurologists were calculated using the ACGME Data Resource Book 2007–2021, 28 publicly available data, CNS membership data, and estimates from previously published surveys. 27 , 38 , 39 The percentage of women within the CNS membership has been stable over the past several years, with 43% of women members according to the 2015 CNS Workforce survey 39 and 41% (1,181/2,875) of women according to the 2023 membership data. Leadership Positions Between 1972 and 2023, 13% (4/32) of presidents, 36% (5/14) of secretary-treasurers, and 33% (35/106) of councilors were women ( Table 1 ). We assessed the total number of positions by gender and decade and the cumulative number of BOD positions by gender over time ( Figure 1A ). In addition, we evaluated the disparity in BOD positions between genders over time ( Figure 1B ). While the disparity gap has narrowed in the past decade, the past 4–6 years show a downward trend in the difference of BOD positions between genders. Table 1. Gender Distribution of Physician Leadership Positions in the CNS by Decade Overall President Secretary-treasurer Councilor Men Women Men Women Men Women Men Women n % n % n % n % n % n % n % n % 1974–1979 25 86 4 14 8 100 0 0 3 100 0 0 14 78 4 22 1980–1989 26 84 5 16 6 86 1 14 3 75 1 25 17 85 3 15 1990–1999 21 78 6 22 5 100 0 0 1 50 1 50 15 75 5 25 2000–2009 14 52 13 48 3 60 2 40 0 0 2 100 11 55 9 45 2010–2019 20 74 7 26 4 80 1 20 2 100 0 0 14 70 6 30 2020–2023 2 18 9 82 2 100 0 0 0 0 1 100 0 0 8 100 Overall 108 71 44 29 28 88 4 13 9 64 5 36 71 67 35 33 Open in a new tab Abbreviation: CNS = Child Neurology Society. Figure 1. Gender Representation of Board of Director Positions in CNS. Open in a new tab (A) Cumulative distribution of board of director positions by gender. (B) The cumulative difference in men and women board of directors each year over time. BOD = board of director; CNS = Child Neurology Society. Women are more likely to hold secretary-treasurer (36%) or councilor (33%) positions than presidential positions (13%, 95% CI 2%–52%, 2-sided p -value < 0.029). From 2003 to 2023, the percentage of women holding these nonpresidential positions has increased compared with becoming president. In 2019, with approval from the BOD, the CNS president directed the nominating committee to explicitly consider gender diversity in reviewing nominees. Despite the increase of women in child neurology and the increase in the representation of women in nonpresidential leadership positions, the overall gap in gender representation in leadership positions remains stable mainly ( Table 2 ). Table 2. Gender Disparity of Women Physician Leaders and Child Neurologists Year % Women physician leaders in CNS % Women child neurologists The absolute disparity between % women child neurologists and % women leaders Two-sided p value b 2002 19.1 (18 of 94) 31.4 (256 of 815) 38 12.3 0.014 2014 24.4 (31 of 127) 35.3 (219 of 620) 39 10.9 0.0003 2023 28.9 (44 of 152) 41.2 (1,181 of 2,875) a 12.3 <0.00001 Open in a new tab Abbreviation: CNS = Child Neurology Society. a 2023 Membership data, personal communication Monique Terrell. b Significance <0.05. Societal Comparisons We compared the gender representation of women presidents in the CNS, AAN, and AAP ( Figure 2 ) with the graph starting in 1972. Before this, there were no women presidents. The gap between men and women in presidential leadership for all 3 societies has grown. The gender difference of presidential leadership in the AAN and AAP has plateaued since 2019, with their last few presidents being women. The AAN had 8% (3/37) of women presidents from 1948 to 2023, with its first in 2003. The AAP had 13% (12/93) from 1930 to 2023, with its first women president in 1990. Figure 2. Gender Disparity of Presidential Leadership of 3 Societies. Open in a new tab Cumulative difference in presidential positions held by men and women each year over time for each society. AAN = American Academy of Neurology; AAP = American Academy of Pediatrics; CNS = Child Neurology Society. Award Recipients Women represented 26% (61/236) of the award recipients. We assessed the total number of awards by gender, nomination process, and decade ( Table 3 ), the cumulative number of award recipients by gender, and the gender difference over time ( Figure 3, A and B ). When we performed an exact binomial test for the proportion of awardees as being 50% women, we found that women represented 23% (95% CI 12%–38%) of the proportion of awardees and were underrepresented (2-sided, p < 0.00001). Women are less likely to receive the Brumback Lifetime Achievement or Hower Awards. Although not a requirement, 47% of Hower award recipients were past presidents (21 men, 2 women). Table 3. Gender Distribution of Award Recipients Overall 2020–2023 2010–2019 2000–2009 1990–1999 1980–1989 1974–1979 Men Women Men Women Men Women Men Women Men Women Men Women Men Women n % n % n % n % n % n % n % n % n % n % n % n % n % n % Awards committee (nomination, no rubric) Martha Bridge Denckla 2 67 1 33 2 67 1 33 Gold 9 69 4 31 1 33 2 67 8 80 2 20 Brumback 31 84 6 16 7 88 1 13 15 83 3 17 9 82 2 18 Bernard Sachs 37 80 9 20 2 67 1 33 8 80 2 20 7 70 3 30 8 80 2 20 9 90 1 10 3 100 0 0 Hower 42 86 7 14 4 100 0 0 6 60 4 40 10 100 0 0 8 89 1 11 8 80 2 20 6 100 0 0 Overall 121 82 27 18 16 76 5 24 37 77 11 23 26 84 5 16 16 84 3 16 17 85 3 15 9 100 0 0 Awards committee (application with rubric) Philip Dodge YIA 29 71 12 29 1 25 3 75 8 80 2 20 7 70 3 30 9 82 2 18 4 67 2 33 Non–awards committee Training director 7 64 4 36 1 33 2 67 6 75 2 25 Bernard D''Souza 18 49 19 51 1 50 1 50 8 57 6 43 6 60 4 40 3 30 7 70 0 0 1 100 Overall 25 52 23 48 2 40 3 60 14 64 8 36 6 60 4 40 3 30 7 70 0 0 1 100 Open in a new tab YIA = Young Investigator Award. Figure 3. Gender Representation of Award Recipients in CNS. Open in a new tab (A) Cumulative distribution of award recipients by gender over time. (B) The cumulative difference between men and women award recipients each year over time. CNS = Child Neurology Society. Nominations We assessed the gender diversity of aggregated nominees for both awards and BOD positions ( Table 4 ). Women were underrepresented in every award except the Dodge Award. However, unlike the other nominated awards, the Dodge is awarded to an investigator who applies for the award. For the Dodge, 54% (14/26) of the applications from 2018 to 2022 were women. Overall, women represented 28% (13/46) of the aggregated award nominees handled by the Awards Committee from 2018 to 2022. Table 4. Gender Distribution of Award Nominations Men Women n % n % Award nominations (2018–2022) Martha Bridge Denckla 4 80 1 20 Gold 4 67 2 33 Brumback 10 77 3 23 Bernard Sachs 7 70 3 30 Hower 8 67 4 33 Overall 33 72 13 28 Award applications (2019–2023) Philip Dodge YIA 12 46 14 54 BOD nominations (2020–2023) President 6 75 2 25 Secretary-treasurer 2 67 1 33 Councilor 14 39 22 61 Overall (noncouncilor) 8 73 3 27 Overall (with councilor) 22 47 25 53 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: BOD = board of director; YIA = Young Investigator Award. For BOD nominations over the past 4 election cycles (2020–2023), women represented 53% (25/47) of nominees in these elections and 27% (3/11) of the noncouncilor nominees. Most of the presidential nominees, 75% (6/8), and secretary-treasurer nominees, 67% (2/3), were men while most of the councilor nominees were women, 61% (22/36). Discussion Women have comprised most of the residents entering child neurology since at least 2007; however, the equity in recognition for women within the CNS has yet to match the same pace. The most considerable worry is that this disparity represents an inherent gender bias within our professional organizations. Noting that inherent bias can be humbling to acknowledge and address, there may be viewpoints that seek to rationalize this disparity otherwise. We address these potential viewpoints by topic and provide suggestions for improving diversity, equity, and inclusion for leadership roles and awards. Seniority in Awards and Leadership In many recognition awards and leadership positions, the CNS tends to value service length to the child neurology field. However, the required years of service eligibility are primarily undefined. Many awards and positions require a nomination, with the requirements interpreted by the awards or nominating committee and election. Two “Legacy” awards, the Roger and Mary Brumback Lifetime Achievement (Brumback) and the Hower Award, had similar lifetime representation of women recipients with 16% (6 of 37) and 14% (7 of 49), respectively, with most nominees being men in the past 5 years. Despite increasing women''s presence on the BOD positions, only 13% (4 of 32) of CNS presidents have been women, the last of whom was in 2013. To fit this topic, a modest goal would be equity in awards and leadership to match the gender distribution within the CNS in the next 10 years. While gender disparity in BOD positions has improved over the past 5 years, more change is needed. The gender disparity of awardees remains apparent. Suggested strategies are as follows: Review eligibility criteria for each award and leadership position to determine the necessity of revisions, including nonbiased language, promotion of equitable membership representation, and objective evaluation criteria. 29 Create targeted awards for early career members, women, and underrepresented minorities. Gender Diversity of the Committees The data for committee representation were limited, although we acknowledge the importance of gender diversity. Social identity theory and in-group favoritism suggest that the general tendency to prefer men over women for leadership positions depends on the decision maker’s gender. When men dominate the decision-making bodies, they tend to favor their in-group (other men). 40 The current awards committee includes 60% women (9 of 15 members, including a woman chair and 1 of 2 Philip Dodge Young Investigator Award recipients). The past president always chairs the nominating committee, which is currently equally represented by men and women. Suggested strategies are as follows: Maintain professional society accountability by transparent publication of the gender, race, and ethnicity distributions of award and leadership nominees and recipients, committee members, plenary speakers, and all other invited positions. 25 Ensure policies promote equitable representation of all genders and underrepresented and sexual individuals who identify with other minority groups in leadership, awards, and committees. Educate nominating and awards committee members on best practices regarding diversity, evaluation, and selection process. 29 The Nomination Process Women are underrepresented in awards and noncouncilor BOD nominations. Although the gender of the nominator was not available to the authors, it is possible that there was concordance. Women are less likely to self-nominate, 31 and self-nominations can affect their likability. 41 Rather than relying on a peer nomination process, the Leadership, Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion (LDEI) committee could seek and submit names of qualified women to the awards committee for each award category annually. The CNS bylaws do not guide nominee review by the awards or nominating committees, selection of candidates for voting, or award recipients, leaving margin for conscious and unconscious biases. Until recently, the process for nomination required the candidate''s CV and candidacy statement for BOD positions or 1–3 letters of recommendation (LORs) for awards. Additional information can still be requested, although these details are unclear. The language used in LOR may reinforce gender stereotypes and roles. The use of first names, hedging language (uncertainty), doubt raisers (potentially negative), and grindstone adjectives (implying success through effort rather than ability) are more often applied to women in LOR. 42 , 43 This language can negatively affect how women are perceived by their reviewers. Before nominating our peers, we can use gender bias calculators or minimize gender language in the candidacy statement before submitting it. In addition, steps should be taken to mitigate unconscious bias, including the potential blinding of reviewers. Suggested strategies are as follows: Perform a gap analysis in the current nomination process, particularly for the more senior and prestigious awards in which fewer women physicians were nominated, and create a plan to recruit/close any gaps actively. Check gender bias language in writing. Encourage increased nominations for women while openly recognizing that gender stereotypes influence our idea of a leader. 30 Evaluate and create a process for identifying an inclusive pool of qualified candidates for awards and BOD positions, acknowledging that women may not self-nominate. 31 Charge the LDEI to submit the names of qualified women and individuals from underrepresented racial/ethnic minority groups to the awards committee for each award category every year. 31 Blinding of reviewers during the award and leadership nominee selection process. Candidate Bias As mentioned previously, both conscious and unconscious biases can affect voting. Both men and women academicians are significantly more likely to positively evaluate a man''s research, teaching, and service contributions than that of an identically qualified woman. 44 Many professional societies use data on the number of associate professors or professors in their specialty to measure qualified candidates; however, these databases lack information on child neurology or are behind paywalls. The Sidney Carter Award in Child Neurology has been presented at the AAN to an individual for their outstanding work in child neurology or developmental neurobiology. Since its conception in 1998, 76% (13 of 18) of recipients have been women. Of those 13 women, 31% (4 of 13) held BOD or have been award recipients within the CNS and 69% (9 of 13) have been CNS members. Comparatively, of the men recipients, 100% (5 of 5) were also CNS members and 80% (4 of 5) held BOD or have been award recipients within the CNS. More broadly, when women are underrepresented, these lost opportunities affect promotion and tenure, ranking, salary, and distinguishment. 45 Notably, a diverse workforce improves the scientific quality, innovation, and progress for all. 25 , 26 Suggested strategies are as follows: Implicit bias training for all BOD and committee members. 32 , 33 Structural Discrimination The bylaws do not explicitly discriminate against others; however, structural sexism exists when policies, procedures, and organizational culture predict specific outcomes, such as the gender disparities described in this article. Nearly half of the CNS members are junior or medical students; however, a smaller percentage attend the annual meeting. Accessibility and affordability to the annual meeting are essential for career development. Those who want to experience the meeting live, not on-demand at a later time, must be able to afford it, take time off, and arrange childcare to attend. In addition, there are minimal scholarship opportunities for individuals from underrepresented racial/ethnic minority groups and junior or medical student members presenting. This can result in lost opportunities to attend, present, network, and build the reputation required for the recognition awards and leadership positions within the CNS. Suggested strategies are as follows: Simplify the membership process for medical students and junior members. Create a process for identifying an inclusive pool of qualified speakers for the annual meeting and ensure ongoing equitable representation. 34 Offer live-hybrid annual meetings to increase accessibility and diversity of speakers and attendees. Increase scholarship opportunities for individuals from underrepresented racial/ethnic minority groups, sexual orientation or gender identity minority groups, and junior or medical student members presenting at the annual meeting to increase accessibility. Examine how proposed bylaw changes affect diversity and inclusion at various organizational levels. 35 Gender Role Stereotypes As previously discussed, the leaky (pathway) theory fails to account for alternative and nonlinear career paths, gender bias, individual beliefs, and broader inequities. 16 , 17 Childcare responsibilities and work-life balance depend on an individual''s gender role beliefs. Although the leaky (pathway) is a common argument, evidence shows that family or lifestyle choices do not adequately account for gender-related disparities. 5 , 34 , 46 In faculty, women with children had less institutional support, less research funding, fewer publications, slower perceived career progress, and lower career satisfaction than men with children. 8 Gender bias against women with children led to exclusion from advancement opportunities by their leadership, despite gendered specific-performance expectations and financial inequities. 47 , 48 Organizations such as the CNS can do their part by providing supportive environments and policies that promote work-family balance for all genders. Suggested strategies are as follows: Adopt a strategy similar to that of the National Institute of Mental Health to support candidates who have taken time away from research for young investigator awards or career development programs. 34 Promote a family-friendly environment at conferences. 36 Moving Toward Equity The CNS has made efforts to increase the diversity of its workforce and infrastructure; however, as a society, we need to do better. Although representation of women has grown over time, particularly in nonpresident leadership positions, gender disparities continue within the CNS. Women need equitable representation in the CNS, and change is necessary. The CNS created the LDEI committee in 2021, recognizing the need to strategize moving toward a more inclusive CNS. 49 The committee aimed to send a message of the ongoing dedication of CNS leadership to confronting and addressing racism and inequity. 50 Addressing ongoing gender disparities and promoting diverse leadership at all career stages should be a part of that inclusive strategy. Some members have concerns about gender representation and ageism, which also affects the CNS membership and participation. Overall, broader structural issues are contributing to gender bias and disparities that occur throughout all stages of academia. As a leader in child neurology, the CNS can create policies and practices that support equitable environments and urge others to do the same. Suggested strategies are as follows: Maintain professional society accountability and transparency by publishing membership data, including diversity distributions and ongoing efforts toward inclusion as internal and external committee reports that can be shared with CNS members. 35 Maintain a commitment to investigate and implement meaningful change in gender disparities. Leaders in several fields, including neurology, have suggested a 6-step evidenced-based plan: (1) examine gender diversity data, (2) transparently report results to members and stakeholders, (3) investigate potential causes of underrepresentation, (4) implement strategies to improve inclusion, (5) track outcomes, and (6) publish results. 6 Engage existing CNS committees, including the LDEI committee. Limitations Data availability proved challenging, including specific gender distribution in child neurology, which was unavailable from the AAN and Association of American Medical Colleges. While the American Board of Psychiatry and Neurology did not start collecting gender data until 2021, deidentified aggregate data were not obtainable. Gender data may be obtained through the American Medical Association; however, the cost was a barrier. Although the CNS has collected gender data since its inception, options for members to self-select gender are limited and were not required. Gender distribution of membership required manual data review that the CNS national office did not permit. We used previously published workforce surveys, which pose limitations given response rates. Finally, the study group wanted to examine race and ethnicity distribution; however, these data must be collected with membership data. Similarly, we did not have complete access to gender distribution of nominees for awards or leadership positions outside the years presented here. These data relied on the previous committee chairs'' record keeping and retention and availability to be included. Gender distribution of the nominator was not available but is an essential consideration in future work. In addition, there is consideration of confidentiality to protect the nominee''s identities. However, we suggest that regular release of the membership and nominee demographics data to society leaders and members, including gender, race, and ethnicity, would be valuable to ensure a diverse selection pool of candidates. It is important to note that gender is fluid and not binary. In addition, some individuals opted not to indicate their gender. An option may have yet to be available to capture their identity, or they may have preferred not to answer. Moreover, the abstraction of gender was not confirmed by direct communication with the award recipient or leader. These results were abstracted from the records of a single professional society and may not be representative of child neurology gender equity in awards and leadership at large. Institutional and regional leadership positions may or may not demonstrate similar disparities. Finally, not all child neurologists are members of the CNS, and thus, this study may not represent the larger community of child neurologists. However, this work could be a starting point for a broader conversation of inequities within child neurology. Conclusions Women remain underrepresented in leadership roles and awards within the CNS. Women have comprised most of the residents entering child neurology since at least 2007; however, disparities persist. Reasons for disparities are known to be multifactorial and likely include gender bias and structural sexism. The CNS must commit to adopting targeted strategies to mitigate these disparities. In addition, where there is gender inequity, other inequities likely exist, and there is a need to look carefully at the representation of individuals who identify with other minorities. As the society for child neurologists, CNS can create equitable policies and practices, set the standard for equitable representation, and advocate for others to do the same, moving toward a more inclusive field of child neurology. Acknowledgment The authors thank the CNS for providing membership data and AAN membership services for providing the Sidney Carter Child Neurology Award recipient list. The authors thank the reviewers for their thoughtful comments and feedback. The authors also note that this effort has garnered widespread support within the CNS as identified by the author list, which includes the current CNS Executive Director and CEO, 3 CNS past presidents, 3 current CNS Board Members, the current Chair of the LDEI Committee, the immediate past Chair of the CNS Awards Committee, 2 additional current CNS committee chairs, and 6 CNS Award recipients. Glossary AAN American Academy of Neurology AAP American Academy of Pediatrics ACGME Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education BOD board of director CNS Child Neurology Society CV curriculum vitae LDEI Leadership, Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion LOR letter of recommendation Appendix. Authors Name Location Contribution Jaclyn M. Martindale, DO Department of Neurology, Wake Forest University School of Medicine, Winston-Salem, NC Drafting/revision of the manuscript for content, including medical writing for content; major role in the acquisition of data; st