anthropomorphism lacson wiegmann madhavan litman holzinger carrington causability kunstl madumal sonenberg vetere deyoung rajani eraser rationalized lipshitz orasanu ehsan riedl causability acn3 orderings shuffled acn3','Abstract Objective Artificial intelligence (AI)‐based decision support systems (DSS) are utilized in medicine but underlying decision‐making processes are usually unknown. Explainable AI (xAI) techniques provide insight into DSS, but little is known on how to design xAI for clinicians. Here we investigate the impact of various xAI techniques on a clinician''s interaction with an AI‐based DSS in decision‐making tasks as compared to a general population. Methods We conducted a randomized, blinded study in which members of the Child Neurology Society and American Academy of Neurology were compared to a general population. Participants received recommendations from a DSS via a random assignment of an xAI intervention (decision tree, crowd sourced agreement, case‐based reasoning, probability scores, counterfactual reasoning, feature importance, templated language, and no explanations). Primary outcomes included test performance and perceived explainability, trust, and social competence of the DSS. Secondary outcomes included compliance, understandability, and agreement per question. Results We had 81 neurology participants with 284 in the general population. Decision trees were perceived as the more explainable by the medical versus general population ( P < 0.01) and as more explainable than probability scores within the medical population ( P < 0.001). Increasing neurology experience and perceived explainability degraded performance ( P = 0.0214). Performance was not predicted by xAI method but by perceived explainability. Interpretation xAI methods have different impacts on a medical versus general population; thus, xAI is not uniformly beneficial, and there is no one‐size‐fits‐all approach. Further user‐centered xAI research targeting clinicians and to develop personalized DSS for clinicians is needed. Introduction Artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning‐based decision support systems (DSS) are increasingly utilized in healthcare. 1 , 2 Typically, these systems are “black box,” that is, the user does not know how the DSS arrives at its decision. This opaqueness can be a roadblock to effective human‐AI teaming, and thus, explainability is critical to give clinicians insight into the reasoning of AI DSS. The field of explainable artificial intelligence 3 (xAI) has produced numerous methods for inspecting AI models, for example, feature importance, 4 counterfactual reasoning, 5 and decision trees. 6 While recent prior work has begun to design xAI for clinical decision‐making, 7 , 8 , 9 there is insufficient analysis comparing the relative efficacies of xAI approaches. Prior work has shown that, without effective explanations, users are prone to accept incorrect advice from a DSS. 10 Without studying clinical populations, DSS practitioners may deploy xAI that could impair clinician decision‐making. 11 , 12 Prior work has examined xAI techniques in a general population 13 but typically does not include clinicians or often only include a single xAI technique. 14 , 15 Thus, the primary point of this investigation is to determine how multiple xAI techniques affect clinician decision‐making. We ground our study in xAI for neurology, which is a challenging decision‐making specialty and lends itself well to DSS. The outcomes of this study are design principles to inform future studies across medicine. Methods Recruitment of participants The user study was administered via Qualtrics using the mailing list from the Child Neurology Society (CNS) and American Academy of Neurology General Neurology message boards targeting medical students, residents, and attendings. The general population was recruited on Amazon Mechanical Turk as English speakers in the USA between 18 and 65 years of age. Description of conditions Each condition represents one of seven xAI approaches (i.e., decision tree, 6 , 16 , 17 , 18 , 19 , 20 , 21 , 22 counterfactual, 23 , 24 , 25 , 26 feature importance, 27 , 28 , 29 templated language, 30 probability scores, 31 , 32 crowd‐sourced scores, and case‐based 33 , 34 , 35 , 36 explanations, as shown in Table 1 ) and one “no explanation” condition as a control (Fig. 1 ). All participants received the same questions, digital assistant, answer recommendations, and answer choices. Only the recommendations'' explanations differed. All explanations were designed by experts for consistency and quality. However, participants were told the DSS generated the explanations. Table 1. Description of eight explanation conditions included in this study. Explainability conditions Description Decision tree Explanations used a three‐level binary decision tree in which each node corresponds to a verifiable check against the question and leads to an answer‐suggestion. Counterfactual Explanations offered an answer suggestion and a short sentence that states how a change in the question would lead to a different answer. Case‐based Explanations offered a small table of data on other cases and their respective answers, which the robot “used” during training. Feature importance Explanations included a small table of features in the question and the percentage‐weight applied to each feature. Templated language Explanations took the top feature from the feature‐importance table and expressed it as a sentence rather than a percentage (e.g., “I took the child''s age and likely epilepsy into consideration to decide that the first line of treatment would be ethosuximide”). Probability scores Explanations included a table with the percent‐likelihood for each answer condition. Crowd sourced Explanations took the most likely answer from the probability scores condition and expressed it as a sentence rather than a table (e.g., “I think the answer is ethosuximide because 85% of experts chose this treatment.”). No explanation No additional information provided. Open in a new tab Figure 1. Open in a new tab An example of a test question along with the DSS agent''s recommendation is depicted in the top‐left box. Examples of each explanation modality used in this study are depicted in the other boxes. Study procedure After obtaining consent (IRB # H20522 ), participants were randomly assigned using Qualtrics to one of eight different xAI conditions (Table S1 ). The full experimental protocol is available in Figure S1 . The study included 13 patient case‐based multiple‐choice questions on pediatric and adult neurological disorders. The 13 study questions were divided into three phases. Phase I: One introductory question with xAI for training participants. Phase II: A “priming task” of three questions without xAI assistance (akin to the nothing xAI condition in Phase III), followed by a survey on perceived explainability of the DSS. Phase III: The “main study” of nine questions with xAI, followed by posttest surveys. Each question was accompanied by a virtual robot, which was used to garner trust through anthropomorphism, 37 and an answer recommendation, using different xAI conditions. To create these questions, the answer choices, the robot''s recommendations, and the various explanations, we conducted a three‐stage process with a set of xAI experts and a set of neurology experts. The xAI experts trained each of the neurologists in characterizing the types of xAI used in this study. In the first stage of the process, the xAI experts worked iteratively with the first neurologist to design the questions, answers, and explanations of each type. This step included the xAI experts vetting the explanations for internal validity (e.g., the decision tree''s reasoning lined up with the features used in the feature descriptors used in the other forms of xAI; the counterfactual reasoning was consistent with a counterfactual branch of the decision tree, etc.). In the second stage, the questions, answers, and explanations were given to an independent board‐certified neurologist not involved in the first stage. Feedback was provided and the study materials were refined. In the third stage, this process was repeated with another board‐certified neurologist. After this third stage, the study materials were determined by the neurologists to be clinically sound and stratified across complexity levels, and the materials were determined by the xAI experts to be internally valid and reflective of the various xAI methods used. We note that the process differed between creating questions, answers, and explanations for when the robot''s recommendation would be correct versus incorrect. For when the robot provided an incorrect recommendation, we wanted the robot''s reasoning to reveal a flaw to a participant who would be scrutinizing the decision. For example, for the decision tree explanations, the neurologist would exchange one leaf node or decision node with another, resulting in a mistake in the tree''s reasoning. For feature importance, the relative weighting placed on the features would be changed. We note that the design of questions that were incorrect required careful consideration by the neurologists because it is difficult to purposefully invent a rationale (e.g., a decision tree) with a mistake in it in a way that might seem logical to another person, perhaps someone with lesser experience. Further, we had to exclude some questions and answers for which it was difficult to create explanations, such as question–answer pairs that are learned through rote memorization from a textbook based upon established medical practice that is not evidence‐based. Questions in Phases II and III were designed to be of varying difficulty level, easy, medium, and hard, which were to be answerable by medical students, residents, and attendings, respectively. DSS recommendations and their explanations were incorrect 31% of the time (Phase I: 1/1 correct; II: 2/3 correct; III; 6/9 correct) to test participants'' ability to respond to faulty DSS with xAI. This error rate was tuned empirically 13 and based upon prior work 38 that humans tend to ignore or fully comply with a DSS when it is correct too little or much. Participants were told the correct answer after each question, which afforded participants to detect errors in the DSS. For all questions, participants also self‐report their perceived understanding and their agreement with the robot''s suggestion. At the end of Phase III, participants rate the virtual robot and explanation condition on surveys of trust, perceptions of social competence and anthropomorphism, and perceived explainability. Primary and secondary outcomes Two primary outcomes were measured at the end of the experiment: (1) performance on neurology questions with assistance from different types of explanations (accuracy on nine neurology questions that used different explanation conditions and reliance/compliance with xAI suggestions) and (2) subjective perceptions of different explanation conditions. In addition to the percentage of questions answered correctly in Phase III, we utilized metrics for no‐xAI DSS performance and xAI performance improvement . We assessed no‐xAI DSS performance by the percentage of correctly answered questions in Phase II and the Nothing condition in Phase III (i.e., when participants received DSS recommendations that did not have xAI‐based explanations). An analysis of variance showed that performance across these two sets of questions was not statistically significantly different and were thus pooled. We assessed the xAI performance improvement by measuring the percentage difference between each participants'' Phase III and Phase II scores. After Phase III, three surveys were administered. First, trust was measured by the Negative Attitude toward Robots Scale 39 (score: 12–84). Second, the Godspeed scales measured perceptions of social intelligence/competence and anthropomorphism of the DSS (score: 10–50). Third, the explainability survey 13 measures perceived explainability (score: 14–98). Secondary outcomes were measured for each question: self‐reported understanding of explanations (score: −100 to 100), self‐reported agreement with explanations (score: −100 to 100), and objective compliance with explanations (binary for each question). For compliance, we separately analyzed inappropriate compliance (i.e., accepting bad advice) and inappropriate reliance (i.e., rejecting good advice 40 , 41 , 42 , 43 ). Lastly, we collected prior experience in neurology and prior experience working with AI or robotics in years as demographic factors. Note: To report years of experience, participants could choose an integer from {0, 1, …, 9} or ≥10 years. Statistical analysis We performed descriptive statistics for the participant characteristics. For continuous data, mean and standard deviation were used for normally distributed data, with median and interquartile range for skewed data. Proportions are reported for categorical variables. For primary and secondary outcomes, we conducted multiple linear regression and analysis of variance (ANOVA) across explanation and population conditions to determine if they were statistically significant. If significant, we conducted a Tukey''s HSD post hoc and reported significantly different groups and P ‐values. When a Tukey''s HSD was not possible due to heteroscedasticity in the multiple linear regression, we applied pairwise t ‐tests for unpaired samples with equal/unequal variances using a Holm–Bonferroni method. For secondary outcomes assessing compliance, understanding, and agreement with DSS recommendations, we conducted multiple logistic regression using a Wald''s test for regression significance and an ANOVA to identify significant regressors. These metrics were measured at each question (i.e., a repeated measure), so a mixed‐effects model with a random effect for the participant would be most appropriate. However, these models overfit the data, so a fixed‐effects model was chosen. Lastly, Kendall rank correlation coefficients were computed for correlations not meeting assumptions of Spearman''s method. A Fisher r ‐to‐ z transformation procedure was used for testing differences between two correlations (e.g., between the medical and general populations). Results Participant demographics We recruited 114 participants (i.e., members of the Child Neurology Society and American Academy of Neurology) for the randomized study, of which 33 did not complete the survey (Fig. 2 ). The final study included a total of 81 participants (mean age = 38.23, SD = 13.09, 30 male, 51 female). Participants reported a median of 7 years'' experience in neurology (IQR = 7) and a median of 0 years'' experience with AI or robotics (IQR = 2). The general population includes 284 participants (mean age: 43.0; SD: 10.7; 137 male, 147 female) recruited via Amazon Mechanical Turk. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Flow of participants through trial. Primary outcomes Medical population Figure 3 and Table 2 depict key findings of our analysis. Supporting the validity of our neurology test questions, we find that neurology experience was a predictor of no‐xAI DSS performance ( R 2 = 0.126, F (1, 89) = 14.0, P < 0.001, Fig. 3a ). Figure 3. Open in a new tab This figure highlights key results from the study. (A) Shows that performance of medical participants without xAI‐based DSS increases with neurology experience. (D) Shows the interaction between neurology experience and perceived explainability of the DSS'' recommendations in explaining test performance (Phase III) relative to performance in the no‐xAI priming phase (Phase II) (B, C, E, F) show the performance (B), trust in the DSS (C), perceived explainability (E), and perceived social competence (F) of medical and general population participants as a function of xAI method used by the DSS. Table 2. The performance (sample mean and SD) of participants across populations and xAI (explainable AI) conditions and phases are reported above. Population xAI condition Phase I Phase II Phase III Sample mean Sample SD Sample mean Sample SD Sample mean Sample SD Medical Decision tree 100 0.00 66.6 29.8 74.7 11.2 Crowd sourced 100 0.00 69.4 30 73.6 10.5 Case‐based 100 0.00 76.1 25.1 73 20.1 Templated language 80.0 42.1 60 26.2 78.8 11.0 Feature importance 100 0.00 77.7 23.5 74.7 10.7 Nothing 90.0 31.6 76.6 27.4 76.6 18.4 Counterfactual 100 0.00 62.5 21.3 73.6 15.6 Probability scores 100 0.00 76.1 27.5 73 15.5 General Decision tree 93.9 24.2 91.5 12.2 75.9 12.3 Crowd sourced 92.5 26.6 86.6 14.6 75.9 11.2 Case‐based 91.4 28.4 89.7 11.2 77.5 10.6 Templated language 97.5 15.6 84.8 13.9 76.1 13.7 Feature importance 90.4 29.7 89 12.6 78.9 11.4 Nothing 89.4 31.1 86.3 13.2 75.5 10.7 Counterfactual 92.3 26.9 86.6 13.2 73.6 10.5 Probability scores 75.8 43.5 88.9 12.6 77.8 10.3 Open in a new tab A multiple linear regression model (Fig. 3d ) for xAI performance improvement as a function of prior experience with AI interaction and interaction effects between perceived explainability and experience in neurology was significant ( R 2 = 0.107, F (4, 66) = 3.09, P = 0.0214). The interaction effect between explainability and neurology experience was significant, showing that participants'' xAI performance improvement degraded when neurology experience and perceived explainability were high (Estimate = −0.3318, 95% CI [−0.640 to −0.024]). As shown in Figure 3d , participants with 1σ less experience than the average neurologist in our study benefited from xAI and increasingly so as the lesser experienced neurologist found the xAI recommendations more explainable. However, participants with 1σ more experience than the average neurologist were hampered by xAI when their DSS agent''s xAI was perceived to be highly explainable. In fact, sub‐selecting for the participants whose explainability ratings were the highest among participants with at least 10 years of neurology experience, we found that their xAI performance degradation was on average 11.1% (SD = 31.3%). We did not find that performance was dependent upon individual xAI mode. However, as shown in Figure 3b , we did find that participants'' scores in the nothing condition (i.e., not xAI‐based) were the second highest (76.7%), with templated language (78.9%) receiving the highest performance on average. The crowed‐sourced xAI condition was lowest (66.7%). We found that the perceived explainability of DSS recommendations was a function of the xAI mode and the participants'' years of neurology experience ( R 2 = 0.2097, F (8, 72) = 3.653, P = 0.00127, Fig. 3e ). Decision trees (contrast = 24.0, 95% CI [7.64–40.3], P = 0.0005), case‐based (contrast = 19.7, 95% CI [0.905–38.5], P = 0.333), and feature importance (contrast = 18.3, 95% CI [0.758–35.69], P = 0.0347), were viewed as more explainable than probability scores. Medical versus general population Unlike for the medical population where the Nothing condition showed the second‐ best Phase III score, the Nothing condition in the general population study achieved the second‐ worst Phase III score (mean = 75.6%) next to counterfactual reasoning (mean = 73.6%). Perceived explainability for decision trees was higher (contrast = 11.0, 95% CI [5.51–16.6], P ‐value = 0.00184) for the medical population as compared to the general population, as shown by post hoc t ‐tests with a Holm–Bonferroni method (Fig. 3e ). The medical population had less trust in the DSS across xAI conditions as compared to our general population ( R 2 = 0.205, F (1, 363) = 95.07, P < 0.0001, Fig. 3c ). A Holm–Bonferroni method with post hoc t‐tests showed that trust decreased from the medical to general populations for all xAI conditions ( P < 0.05). Our medical population viewed all xAI conditions as less socially competent than the general population ( R 2 = 0.1064, F (1, 363) = 44.34, P < 0.0001, Fig. 3f ). A Holm–Bonferroni method with post hoc t‐tests showed that case‐based reasoning (contrast = −8.20, 95% CI [−12.4 to −4.03], P ‐value = 0.00228), probability scores (contrast = −6.90, 95% CI [−10.9 to −2.87], P ‐value = 0.00904), and templated language (contrast = −4.619, 95% CI [−7.56 to −1.68], P ‐value = 0.0164) were viewed as less socially competent by the medical population versus the general population. Secondary outcomes for all phase III questions A logistic regression model was significant for compliance when including explanatory variables for perceived explainability, neurology experience, agreement with the DSS agent and whether the DSS agent''s recommendation was correct along with interaction effects between explainability and neurology experience as well as neurology experience and agreement with the DSS agent ( χ 2 (7) = 218.3, P < 0.0001). This model showed that increasing agreement with the DSS agent''s recommendations increased a participant''s compliance with that recommendation (OR = 1.62, 95% CI [0.187–14.2]). Participants were less likely to comply with incorrect recommendations (OR = 0.0669, 95% CI [0.0408–0.107]). Lastly, there was a slight but significant interaction effect showing that increasing neurology experience and agreement with the recommendations implied an increased likelihood of compliance (OR = 1.002, 95% CI [1.001–1.003]). A logistic regression model for inappropriate compliance was significantly explained by the interaction of neurology experience and agreement with the DSS agent''s advice ( χ 2 (4) = 53.0, P < 0.0001). Increasing neurology experience (OR = 0.860, 95% CI [0.742–0.986]) decreased rates of inappropriate compliance whereas both agreement with bad advice (OR = 1.010, 95% CI [1.000–1.022]) and when someone with more neurology experience agreed with bad advice, that is, an interaction effect, (OR = 1.001, 95%CI [1.000–1.003] increased rates of inappropriate reliance. A logistic regression model for inappropriate reliance was significantly explained by interactions between neurology experience, agreement with the DSS agent''s advice, and explainability ( χ 2 (4) = 24.8, P < 0.0001). The interaction effect showed that participants were slightly but significantly less likely to accept sound advice from a DSS agent when those participants were more experienced in neurology and found the advice more explainable (OR = 1.0001, 95% CI [1.000–1.0002]). Medical versus general population When pooling data across the medical and general population experiments, a logistic regression model showed that compliance was explained by the xAI condition, understanding, neurology experience, agreement, whether the DSS agent''s recommendations were correct, the population, and interaction effects for neurology with agreement along with recommendation correctness and the population ( χ 2 (8) = 445, P < 0.0001). Understanding of the advice (OR = 0.997, 95% CI [0.994–0.999]), neurology experience (OR = 0.826, 95% CI [0.762–0.892]), and the population being medical (OR = 0.917, 95% CI [0.562–1.538]), and when the DSS recommended incorrect advice (OR = 0.129, 95% CI [0.107, 0.154]) all lowered the likelihood of compliance. Agreement with the recommendation increased the likelihood of compliance (OR = 1.01, 95% CI [1.01,1.01]). For the interaction effects, we found again that increasing neurology experience in the presence of higher agreement with the recommendation resulted in higher rates of compliance (OR = 1.002, 95% CI [1.001–1.003]). Second, when the advice was incorrect and the participant was in the medical population, participants were less likely to comply with the advice (OR = 0.534, 95% CI [0.318–0.881]). We note that the population was included as an individual term as it lowered the AIC and was significant for the noted interaction effect. We found that the correlation between agreement and compliance was stronger (Fisher''s z = 5.30, P < 0.0001) in the medical population ( τ = 0.552, z = 21, P < 0.0001) than the general population ( τ = 0.416, z = 37.1, P < 0.0001). However, we found that the correlation between understanding and agreement was weaker (Fisher''s z = −8.69, P < 0.0001) with the medical population ( τ = 0.537, z = 24.0, P < 0.0001) than in the general population ( τ = 0.712, z = 74.9, P < 0.0001). Discussion In this study, we found several key drivers explaining decision‐making performance across a variety of xAI techniques and in comparing a medical versus general population. These results fill in a critical gap as previous xAI research has focused only on AI experts 44 , 45 or the general population 13 , 46 and often includes only one type of xAI. 47 Negatively, we found that xAI degraded the relative performance of participants with the most neurology experience and who found their xAI recommendations to be more explainable (Fig. 3d ). Positively, xAI improved the performance of the participants with the least neurology experience, and that benefit increased in proportion to the participants'' perceived explainability of the method. Nonetheless, it is unclear that test (i.e., Phase III) performance in aggregate was improved with xAI‐based DSS compared to no‐xAI‐based DSS (i.e., nothing). We also note that participants'' performance in the Nothing condition in both studies outperformed the accuracy of the DSS agent on Phase III questions for the medial and general population studies, respectively. We hypothesize that this difference is due to participants being able to identify when the DSS agent was in error on easier questions, without the confound of xAI, and subsequently overriding that recommendation. Recognition‐primed decision‐making (RPDM) may help explain our results, as RPDM suggests that experts do not perform rational, algorithmic decision‐making but instead perform black box pattern recognition. 48 , 49 We hypothesize that novice neurologists benefited from xAI when perceived as more explainable because they were still relying on algorithms and rule‐based thinking taught as a part of medical school pedagogy. Based upon our analysis of inappropriate reliance and compliance, we hypothesize that when experienced neurologists erred, it was more likely due to being tricked by compelling xAI into accepting the DSS agent''s incorrect recommendation (i.e., inappropriate reliance). In turn, we hypothesize that expert neurologists may have found the xAI dissimilar to how they make decisions, in line with RPDM research. Experts may have been more likely to disregard the agents'' advice when viewed as unexplainable. Yet, when the advice was more explainable, experts were potentially parsing the DSS'' recommendations with the consequence that the xAI may have disrupted the experts'' pattern recognition process. Further research is needed to understand whether xAI degrades naturalistic decision‐making in medicine and what form of xAI may support this decision‐making process across experience levels. We found that the medical population had significantly lower self‐reported trust in AI agents. Thus, it may be more difficult for researchers to develop DSS tools that are accepted by a clinician. In the medical versus general populations, Decision Trees were viewed as more explainable and Probability scores less explainable. One participant who worked with probability scores said, “The robot never explained WHY it provided the answer…” This highlights that confidence scores are insufficient for xAI. 50 Further, we found that explanations impact the likelihood that a human will understand, agree with, and comply with the AI''s advice, 10 , 13 and these impacts were not the same for the medical and general populations. Correlations between self‐reported agreement and compliance were significantly stronger in the medical versus the general population, suggesting that medical users of DSS are more discerning when considering AI advice (i.e., less likely to accept advice with which they disagree). These results underscores the need for improved understanding of how clinicians perceive and use xAI, and to develop xAI techniques to accurately calibrate human trust and compliance in each application domain. 50 Our results also demonstrate the need for personalization in xAI‐based DSS. In both the medical and general population, perceived explainability was a more important factor than the specific xAI condition when predicting participant responses. Perceived explainability and neurology experience had a statistically significant interaction effect on participants'' xAI performance improvement, but the individual xAI condition did exhibit this interaction effect. We hypothesize that a persons'' ability to intuit the explanation (i.e., perceived explainability) is a performance driver; thus, xAI DSS designers must develop adaptive or adaptable systems to account for this personalization effect. Based upon these results, we provide three key take‐aways: xAI can improve or degrade performance of medical professionals based upon degree of professional experience and the perceived explainability of the DSS. Future work needs to reconcile current efforts in xAI with naturalistic decision‐making processes of clinicians. Our medical and general populations community reacted differently to xAI both overall and per condition. Thus, xAI DSS tools should be developed in partnership with clinicians rather than relying on a population of convenience for making xAI‐related design choices. For example, medical professionals view decision trees significantly more favorably in comparison to the general population. xAI has a person‐specific, subjective, and objective impact on clinicians. As such, DSS design must be adaptable to the needs of each end‐user rather than a one‐size‐fits‐all design. This will require additional research and cost in development but is critical for improving outcomes. Limitations Explanations in this study were handcrafted by experts as using actual xAI algorithms would have introduced confounds based upon the specific algorithmic implementations and training datasets. Because of the numerous types and variants of xAI, we thought it prudent to distill the essence of each approach in a general way by handcrafting representations of each method for our study. Otherwise, our results would be outdated as soon as a newer, better implementation of a specific deep learning‐based xAI system was published. Our manual approach avoids these confounds. Nonetheless, employing automated xAI techniques in our study would have enhanced the external validity of our study and provided a clearer understanding of which automated xAI techniques would best support end users today. As such, future work can include comparing the fidelity and efficacy of our handcrafted explanations to those generated by state‐of‐the‐art, automated xAI techniques. Further, our study is focused on neurologists and may differ from other specialties. Finally, this survey relies on relatively few diagnostic scenarios despite covering a breadth of topics that from commonly asked questions on the neurology shelf and board exams. Conclusions We demonstrate the different objective and subjective impacts of xAI on neurologists and the need for personalization in xAI‐based DSS. More explainable forms of xAI may disrupt expert neurologists'' decision‐making whereas the opposite is true for novice neurologists. The medical population was less trusting of xAI. Our study suggests that results from a general population cannot be extrapolated to the medical community. For example, the performance of expert neurologists was relatively hampered by xAI when explanations were perceived as more explainable whereas performance improved for less‐experienced participants. Further, the relative objective and subjective benefits of various xAI methods were not consistent across the populations. Thus, research must reexamine several key results across the human–machine interaction 37 and user acceptance modeling communities 51 , 52 to consider population‐specific factors to develop guidelines for the application of xAI to DSS in clinical decision‐making. Author contributions G.G. and M.C.G.: Conception or design of the work; and acquisition, analysis, or interpretation of data for the work; and drafting the work or revising it critically for important intellectual content; and final approval of the version to be published; and agreement to be accountable for all aspects of the work in ensuring that questions related to the accuracy or integrity of any part of the work are appropriately investigated and resolved and acquired funding for this work. M.S. and A.S.: Conception or design of the work; and acquisition, analysis, or interpretation of data for the work; and drafting the work or revising it critically for important intellectual content; and final approval of the version to be published; and agreement to be accountable for all aspects of the work in ensuring that questions related to the accuracy or integrity of any part of the work are appropriately investigated and resolved. N.G., J.H.C., and M.D.: Conception or design of the work; interpretation of data for the work; and drafting the work or revising it critically for important intellectual content; and final approval of the version to be published; and Agreement to be accountable for all aspects of the work in ensuring that questions related to the accuracy or integrity of any part of the work are appropriately investigated and resolved. Conflict of interest GG receives part time salary support from the Centers for Diseases Control and Prevention for acute flaccid myelitis surveillance and an honorarium as Media Editor for Pediatric Neurology. AS received salary support from Apple Inc. as a part of an Apple Scholars in AI/ML fellowship for PhD students. MG receives funding for his lab and salary from the following: Google, Inc., Office of Naval Research, MIT Lincoln Laboratory, National Institutes of Health, National Science Foundation, Ford Motor Company, Sandia National Laboratories, Naval Research Laboratory, National Aeronautics and Space Administration, and Konica Minolta. MG has also received payment as a consultant for the Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Lab and as an expert witness for an injury law firm. JHC, MD, NG, and MS have no disclosures. Supporting information Figure S1. The protocol for this study. ACN3-11-1224-s002.pdf (813.2KB, pdf) Table S1. All neurology questions and answers are included in this study. The xAI DSS'' recommendation is shown in bold. Answer orderings were shuffled for the experiment. For the first column, the designation of “E,” “M,” or “H” stands for easy, medium, and hard. ACN3-11-1224-s001.docx (19.7KB, docx) Acknowledgments None. Funding Information This work was supported by the National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (NCATS) of the National Institutes of Health (NIH) under Award Numbers UL1TR002378 and KL2TR002381 (GG). This work was also supported by a gift from Konica Minolta to the Georgia Tech Research Corporation (MG). Funding Statement This work was funded by National Institutes of Health grants KL2TR002381 and UL1TR002378; Konica Minolta; Georgia Tech Research Corporation. Contributor Information Grace Y. Gombolay, Email: ggombol@emory.edu. Matthew Gombolay, Email: matthew.gombolay@cc.gatech.edu. References 1. Gombolay GY, Gopalan N, Bernasconi A, et al. Review of machine learning and artificial intelligence (ML/AI) for the pediatric neurologist. Pediatr Neurol. 2023;141:42‐51. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Loftus TJ, Altieri MS, Balch JA, et al. Artificial intelligence–enabled decision support in surgery: state‐of‐the‐art and future directions. Ann Surg. 2023;278(1):51‐58. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Samek W, Montavon G, Lapuschkin S, Anders CJ, Müller K‐R. Explaining deep neural networks and beyond: a review of methods and applications. Proc IEEE. 2021;109(3):247‐278. [ Google Scholar ] 4. Sundararajan M, Taly A, Yan Q. Axiomatic attribution for deep networks. PMLR; 2017:3319–3328. 5. Stepin I, Alonso JM, Catala A, Pereira‐Fariña M. A survey of contrastive and counterfactual explanation generation methods for explainable artificial intelligence. IEEE Access. 2021;9:11974‐12001. [ Google Scholar ] 6. Silva A, Gombolay M, Killian T, Jimenez I, Son S‐H. Optimization methods for interpretable differentiable decision trees applied to reinforcement learning. PMLR; 2020:1855–1865. 7. Holzinger A, Langs G, Denk H, Zatloukal K, Müller H. Causability and explainability of artificial intelligence in medicine. Wiley Interdiscip Rev Data Min Knowl Discov. 2019;9(4):e1312. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Klauschen F, Dippel J, Keyl P, et al. Toward explainable artificial intelligence for precision pathology. Annu Rev Pathol. 2024;19:541‐570. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Van der Velden BH, Kuijf HJ, Gilhuijs KG, Viergever MA. Explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) in deep learning‐based medical image analysis. Med Image Anal. 2022;79:102470. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Poursabzi‐Sangdeh F, Goldstein DG, Hofman JM, Wortman Vaughan JW, Wallach H. Manipulating and Measuring Model Interpretability. 2021:1–52. 11. Ghassemi M, Oakden‐Rayner L, Beam AL. The false hope of current approaches to explainable artificial intelligence in health care. Lancet Digit Health. 2021;3(11):e745‐e750. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Rudin C. Stop explaining black box machine learning models for high stakes decisions and use interpretable models instead. Nat Mach Intell. 2019;1(5):206‐215. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Silva A, Schrum M, Hedlund‐Botti E, Gopalan N, Gombolay M. Explainable artificial intelligence: evaluating the objective and subjective impacts of xai on human‐agent interaction. Int J Hum Comput Interact. 2023;39(7):1390‐1404. [ Google Scholar ] 14. Panigutti C, Beretta A, Giannotti F, Pedreschi D. Understanding the impact of explanations on advice‐taking: a user study for AI‐based clinical Decision Support Systems. 2022:1–9. 15. Hansen K, Baehrens D, Schroeter T, Rupp M, Müller KR. Visual interpretation of kernel‐based prediction models. Mol Inform. 2011;30(9):817‐826. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Agarwal N, Das S. Interpretable machine learning tools: a survey. IEEE; 2020:1528–1534. 17. Bastani O, Pu Y, Solar‐Lezama A. Verifiable reinforcement learning via policy extraction. Adv Neural Inf Proces Syst. 2018;31:2499‐2509. [ Google Scholar ] 18. Craven M, Shavlik J. Extracting tree‐structured representations of trained networks. Adv Neural Inf Proces Syst. 1995;8:24‐30. [ Google Scholar ] 19. Wu M, Hughes M, Parbhoo S, Zazzi M, Roth V, Doshi‐Velez F. Beyond sparsity: Tree regularization of deep models for interpretability. 2018. 20. Murthy SK. Automatic construction of decision trees from data: a multi‐disciplinary survey. Data Min Knowl Disc. 1998;2:345‐389. [ Google Scholar ] 21. Paleja R, Niu Y, Silva A, Ritchie C, Choi S, Gombolay M. Learning interpretable, high‐performing policies for continuous control problems. arXiv preprint arXiv:220202352 2022. 22. Paleja R, Silva A, Chen L, Gombolay M. Interpretable and personalized apprenticeship scheduling: learning interpretable scheduling policies from heterogeneous user demonstrations. Adv Neural Inf Proces Syst. 2020;33:6417‐6428. [ Google Scholar ] 23. Wachter S, Mittelstadt B, Russell C. Counterfactual explanations without opening the black box: automated decisions and the GDPR. Harv JL Tech. 2017;31:841. [ Google Scholar ] 24. Karimi A‐H, Barthe G, Schölkopf B, Valera I. A survey of algorithmic recourse: definitions, formulations, solutions, and prospects. arXiv preprint arXiv:201004050 2020. 25. Verma S, Boonsanong V, Hoang M, Hines KE, Dickerson JP, Shah C. Counterfactual explanations and algorithmic recourses for machine learning: a review. arXiv preprint arXiv:201010596 2020. 26. Karimi A‐H, Schölkopf B, Valera I. Algorithmic recourse: from counterfactual explanations to interventions. 2021:353–362. 27. Jain S, Wallace BC. Attention is not explanation. arXiv preprint arXiv:1902.10186 2019. 28. Suau X, Zappella L, Apostoloff N. Finding experts in transformer models. arXiv preprint arXiv:200507647 2020. 29. Ribeiro MT, Singh S, Guestrin C. Why should I trust you? Explaining the predictions of any classifier. 2016:1135–1144. 30. Tambwekar P, Silva A, Gopalan N, Gombolay M. Natural language specification of reinforcement learning policies through differentiable decision trees. IEEE Robot Autom Lett. 2023;8:3621‐3628. [ Google Scholar ] 31. van der Waa J, Schoonderwoerd T, van Diggelen J, Neerincx M. Interpretable confidence measures for decision support systems. Int J Hum‐Comput Stud. 2020;144:102493. [ Google Scholar ] 32. Zhang Y, Liao QV, Bellamy RK. Effect of confidence and explanation on accuracy and trust calibration in AI‐assisted decision making 2020:295–305. 33. Caruana R, Kangarloo H, Dionisio JD, Sinha U, Johnson D. Case‐based explanation of non‐case‐based learning methods. American Medical Informatics Association; 1999:212‐215. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 34. Caruana R, Lou Y, Gehrke J, Koch P, Sturm M, Elhadad N. Intelligible models for healthcare: predicting pneumonia risk and hospital 30‐day readmission. 2015:1721–1730. 35. Koh PW, Liang P. Understanding black‐box predictions via influence functions. PMLR; 2017:1885–1894. 36. Silva A, Chopra R, Gombolay M. Cross‐loss influence functions to explain deep network representations. PMLR; 2022:1–17. 37. Natarajan M, Gombolay M. Effects of anthropomorphism and accountability on trust in human robot interaction. 2020:33–42. 38. Parasuraman R, Manzey DH. Complacency and bias in human use of automation: an attentional integration. Hum Factors. 2010;52(3):381‐410. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 39. Nomura T, Suzuki T, Kanda T, Kato K. Measurement of negative attitudes toward robots. Interacti Stud Soc Behav Commun Biol Artif Syst. 2006;7(3):437‐454. [ Google Scholar ] 40. Azevedo‐Sa H, Jayaraman SK, Esterwood CT, Yang XJ, Robert LP Jr, Tilbury DM. Real‐time estimation of drivers'' trust in automated driving systems. Int J Soc Robot. 2021;13(8):1911‐1927. [ Google Scholar ] 41. Haskins A. An interaction between anthropomorphism and personality on trust in automated systems. 2021. 42. Lacson FC, Wiegmann DA, Madhavan P. Effects of attribute and goal framing on automation reliance and compliance. Sage Publications; 2005:482‐486. [ Google Scholar ] 43. Meyer J. Conceptual issues in the study of dynamic hazard warnings. Hum Factors. 2004;46(2):196‐204. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 44. Hoffman RR, Mueller ST, Klein G, Litman J. Metrics for explainable AI: challenges and prospects. arXiv preprint arXiv:181204608 2018. 45. Holzinger A, Carrington A, Müller H. Measuring the quality of explanations: the system causability scale (SCS) comparing human and machine explanations. KI‐Künstl Intell. 2020;34(2):193‐198. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 46. Madumal P, Miller T, Sonenberg L, Vetere F. Explainable reinforcement learning through a causal lens. 2020;34:2493–2500. [ Google Scholar ] 47. DeYoung J, Jain S, Rajani NF, et al. ERASER: a benchmark to evaluate rationalized NLP models. arXiv preprint arXiv:191103429 2019. 48. Lipshitz R, Klein G, Orasanu J, Salas E. Taking stock of naturalistic decision making. J Behav Decis Mak. 2001;14(5):331‐352. [ Google Scholar ] 49. Klein G. A naturalistic decision making perspective on studying intuitive decision making. J Appl Res Mem Cogn. 2015;4(3):164‐168. [ Google Scholar ] 50. Ehsan U, Riedl MO. Explainability pitfalls: beyond dark patterns in explainable AI. arXiv preprint arXiv:210912480 2021. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 51. Shin D. The effects of explainability and causability on perception, trust, and acceptance: implications for explainable AI. Int J Hum‐Comput Stud. 2021;146:102551. [ Google Scholar ] 52. Davis FD. A Technology Acceptance Model for Empirically Testing New End‐User Information Systems: theory and Results. Massachusetts Institute of Technology; 1985. [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Figure S1. The protocol for this study. ACN3-11-1224-s002.pdf (813.2KB, pdf) Table S1. All neurology questions and answers are included in this study. The xAI DSS'' recommendation is shown in bold. Answer orderings were shuffled for the experiment. For the first column, the designation of “E,” “M,” or “H” stands for easy, medium, and hard. ACN3-11-1224-s001.docx (19.7KB, docx)',7,'2025-10-08 11:31:19','2025-10-08 11:31:19');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(653,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10524612','Audio or Video? Access to Pediatric Neurology Outpatient Services Varies by the Type of Telehealth, Especially for Black Children.','Grefe A, Chen SH, Ip EH, Kirkendall E, Nageswaran S.','2023','reimburse flexibilities flexibilities cancelation bch baptist ahwfb bch bch bch bch ahwfb webex bch bch schedulers bch bch tricare bch geocoding bch bch cancelation bch cancelation cancelation bch cancelation geepack cancelation cancelation cancelation cancelation bch bch cancelation cancelation flexibilities flexibilities flexibilities flexibilities flexibilities kwabena sekyere programmer ostasiewski larisa ctsi ctsi nageswaran ctsi wfbmi ul1tr001420 bch ahwfb baptist ncdhhs uscher pines dworsky e224759 muppavarapu hurd psychiat quart 20claims 20during 20covid 2d19 20visits 20cpt 20codes retroactive 20to 20march ncdhhs ncdhhs flexibilities ncdhhs flexibilities ncdhhs 20bulletin 20covid 2d19 20extension 20nc 20state 20to 20june bluecrossnc medicalpolicy guido chadehumbe helbig stevermer montez amati carolinas rametta fridinger e1257 e1266 eberly kallan julien e2031640 andino betancourt sequist ganguli couture graven kassakian ooab056 skelton halvorson elkhider knottnerus tugwell halekoh hojsgaard geepack berghofer caboral eschbach e73 e82 vishwanath guzek gentry golomb guzek fadel golomb 2329048x211012544 benda veinot sieck ancker klinger carlini','Abstract Background Children of minority race/ethnicity face barriers to accessing specialty services. During the COVID pandemic, health insurance companies reimbursed telehealth services. Our objective was to evaluate the effect of audio vs. video visits on children’s access to outpatient neurology services, particularly for Black children. Methods Using Electronic Health Record data, we collected information about children who had outpatient neurology appointments in a tertiary care children’s hospital in North Carolina from 3/10/20 to 3/9/21. We used multivariable models to compare appointment outcomes (canceled vs. completed, and missed vs. completed) by visit type. We then conducted similar evaluation for the subgroup of Black children. Results A total of 1,250 children accounted for 3,829 scheduled appointments. Audio users were more likely to be Black and Hispanic, and to have public health insurance than video users. Adjusted Odds Ratio (aOR) for appointments completed vs. canceled was 10 for audio and 6 for video, compared to in-person appointments. Audio visits were twice as likely as in-person visits to be completed vs. missed; video visits were not different. For the subgroup of Black children, aOR for appointments completed vs. canceled for audio was 9 and video was 5, compared to in-person appointments. For Black children, audio visits were 3 times as likely as in-person visits to be completed vs. missed; video visits were not different. Conclusions Audio visits improved access to pediatric neurology services, especially for Black children. Reversal of policies to reimburse audio visits could deepen the socio-economic divide for children’s access to neurology services. Keywords: telehealth, children, neurology services, access INTRODUCTION In March 2020, in response to the COVID-19 pandemic, policies were enacted to allow reimbursement of clinicians for telehealth services for the first time in North Carolina (NC) 1 similar to elsewhere in the country. This policy change to allow for reimbursement for telehealth services resulted in increased adoption of telehealth for pediatric specialty care. 2 Since the beginning of the pandemic, neurology providers in our children’s hospital observed that many families were not able to complete telehealth visits based on video platforms (“video visits”) and requested telephone visits (“audio visits”) instead. Additionally, some providers had to switch to audio visits during the attempted video visits because of technical challenges (e.g. poor connectivity, not having access to a video platform etc.). Prior reports have shown that subspecialty audio visits are more likely to be completed than video visits. 3 Since the implementation of telehealth policies, Medicare increased payments for audio visits to match – in-person visits. 4 NC Medicaid reimbursed audio visits, but at a lower level than video visits. 5 There is variability in commercial insurance companies’ coverage of audio visits. 6 While NC Medicaid has now made telehealth flexibilities permanent, 7 commercial insurance companies are evaluating whether to continue telehealth services for their beneficiaries after the end of the public health emergency. 8 Reversal to pre-pandemic reimbursement policies could threaten the availability of audio visits for specialty care for children. While telehealth use has helped reduce problems with access to specialty care services that would not have otherwise been possible during the pandemic, 9 inequities exist for children and adults in telehealth access. 10 , 11 Prior studies have shown that Black patients are less likely to use telehealth for clinic appointments. 11 – 13 There is also substantial evidence that Black adult patients are less likely to use video visits rather than audio visits. 11 , 14 – 18 Previous pediatric and adult studies show that Black patients to be more likely to have had missed outpatient appointments. 19 , 20 If pandemic-related telehealth flexibilities were to be reversed, or if audio visits continue to be reimbursed at a lower rate than video visits, there is a potential for widening disparities in access to pediatric neurology care for children of Black race. Therefore, we sought to evaluate whether children’s access to pediatric neurology outpatient visits (as measured by visit completion, cancelation, and missed appointments) differed by the type of telehealth visit (audio vs. video). In particular, we wanted to evaluate the relationship between telehealth visit type and pediatric neurology access outcomes for children of Black race. METHODS Setting This study was conducted at Brenner Children’s Hospital (BCH), a 181-bed tertiary care children’s hospital within the Atrium Health Wake Forest Baptist (AHWFB) system in Winston-Salem, NC. BCH serves children in a 19-county region in NC, Virginia, South Carolina, and Tennessee. Over 6,000 children are admitted to BCH each year, and more than 44,000 pediatric subspecialty visits occur annually at its outpatient clinics. In the year prior to the pandemic, ~5,100 children received pediatric neurology outpatient care. Of these children, 17% were Black and 12% were Hispanic. The Institutional Review Board of Wake Forest University School of Medicine approved the study. We used an observational cohort study design and followed Strengthening the Reporting of Observational studies in Epidemiology checklist for cohort studies to report our study. 21 Existing demographic and clinical encounter data for children available in the institution’s Electronic Health Record (EHR) was the source of data. Telehealth at BCH Prior to March 2020, telehealth was not provided for children attending neurology clinics at BCH. In March 2020, in response to the COVID pandemic, telehealth services were initiated, and structures and processes to deliver telehealth services were created within the AHWFB system. Video visits were conducted using Doximity or through WebEx within the institution’s EHR, Epic (Epic Systems, Verona, WI). Study Cohort We used a clinical administrative database to identify children who had at least one pediatric neurology outpatient appointment scheduled between 3/10/2019 and 3/9/2020 at BCH. Using children’s medical record numbers, we queried the EHR to identify children eligible for the study. Inclusion criteria were: age <18 years as of 3/10/2019, residence in NC, and at least one pediatric neurology appointment scheduled between 3/10/2020 and 3/9/2021. Pediatric Neurology Outpatient Encounters Encounters were initially identified using search terms for pediatric neurology services within BCH’s pediatrics and neurology departments. Encounter types were limited to the 37 codes that were used by schedulers to create pediatric neurology outpatient appointments, and provided by one of the 18 pediatric neurology providers (9 attending physicians, 3 advanced practice providers and 6 resident physicians) practicing at BCH between 3/10/19 and 3/9/21. Child-level Data Child-level data abstracted from the EHR included date of birth, race, ethnicity, health insurance, and address. Encounter-level data included dates of encounter, type of visit (audio, video, or in-person), and health insurance and address at the time of the encounter. Encounter Data An encounter was coded as a telehealth visit if it was scheduled as an audio visit or a video visit. Data about outcome of appointments (completed, canceled, or missed/no-show) were abstracted. Duplicate encounters (i.e., same type of encounter in a day) were excluded. When there was more than one type of encounter on the same day (e.g., completed and canceled), we used the following rule for including the encounter: completed appointment was considered superior to canceled appointment; and canceled appointment superior to non-show appointment. Variables Age, race, ethnicity, health insurance type, rural/urban residence, and distance from BCH were the demographic variables. Race was categorized as White or Caucasian, Black or African American, and Other. The “Other” category included American Indian, Asian, Latin American, Native Hawaiian and other. Ethnicity was categorized as Hispanic and non-Hispanic. Health insurance was categorized into public and private. Public insurance included Medicaid, Medicare, Tricare, and other government programs. Private insurance included commercial insurance and Managed Care. We used child’s address for residence (rural vs. urban) and to calculate distance to BCH. We used data from a database developed from the American Community Survey based on 2015 geocoding at census tract level 22 and calculated rurality score for each address (0 to 1, with 0 being most rural and 1 being most urban). A rurality score of >0.5 was defined as urban, and a score of ≤ 0.5 as rural. Distance from BCH was calculated using coordinates for BCH and the child’s address, using a custom-built function within the local database developed by the Wake Forest Center for Biomedical Informatics. “Telehealth user” was defined as a child who had at least one telehealth appointment scheduled from 3/10/20 to 3/9/21. “Audio user” and “video user” were those who only had audio visits and video visits respectively for their telehealth encounters. Those who had both audio and video visits for their telehealth encounters were classified as “mixed user”. Type of encounter (audio vs. video vs. in-person) was the primary independent variable of interest. Appointment outcomes - completion, cancelation, and missed - were the outcome variables. Data Analysis Race, ethnicity, health insurance, and rural/urban residence were categorical variables. Age and household distance from BCH were continuous variables. For bivariate analysis, we used demographic characteristics of children at the time of their first scheduled appointment, and compared these characteristics between audio, video, and mixed groups. We used chi-squared tests for categorical variables, and t-tests for continuous variables. We calculated completion, cancelation, and missed rates as the proportion of scheduled appointments that were completed, canceled, and missed respectively, and compared these rates for audio, video, and in-person encounters. For multivariable analysis, we tested 2 models – one comparing appointment cancelation vs. completion, and another comparing appointment missed vs. completion. Both models included the type of encounter (audio, video, or in-person appointment) as the independent variable, and demographic variables as covariates. Age, rural/urban status, distance from BCH, and health insurance data at each encounter were used as time-dependent covariates. Odds ratios for appointment cancelation vs. completion and appointment missed vs. completion, adjusted for all other variables in the model, were calculated. Next, subgroup analysis for children of Black race was performed using multivariable models similar to that described above. However, ethnicity was not used in these models because only three children of Hispanic ethnicity were in this subgroup. For multivariable models, to account for correlation between multiple encounter-level observations per child, we used Generalized Estimating Equations using R package geepack . 23 A p value of <0.05 was considered statistically significant. RESULTS Characteristics of study population A total of 1,250 children who had a telehealth appointment scheduled from 3/10/20 to 3/9/21. Of these, 607 (49%) children were audio users [audio only 206 (16%), and audio and in-person 401 (32%)]; 517 (41%) were video users [video only 182 (15%), and video and in-person 335 (27%)]; and 126 (10%) mixed users [audio and video 25 (2%), and all three types 101 (8%)]. Demographic characteristics of children were as follows: mean age 10.5 years; 63% had public insurance; 67% were White, 17% Black, and 16% Other; 11% were Hispanic; 35% lived in a rural area; and mean distance to the children’s hospital was 31 miles. These 1,250 children accounted for 3,829 pediatric neurology outpatient visits scheduled. Characteristics of audio vs. video user Demographic characteristics of the 3 telehealth user groups (audio, video users and mixed) are compared in Table 1 . Video users were slightly younger than audio users. Audio users were more likely to be Black and Hispanic, and to have public health insurance compared to video users. There was no difference between the groups in the distance from the children’s hospital, and rural vs. urban residence. Table 1. Demographic characteristics of children based on the type of telehealth used All Telehealth Users (n=1250) Audio Users (n=607) Video Users (n=517) Mixed (n=126) p value * Age (mean), years 10.5 (5.1) 11.04 (5.1) 10.0 (5.1) 10.7 (5.2) 0.004 Public Insurance (%) 62.6 69.4 55.3 59.5 <0.0001 Private Insurance (%) 37.1 30.3 44.5 39.7 Missing (%) 0.3 0.3 0.2 0.8 Race categories <0.0001 White (%) 67.1 60.3 73.9 72.2 Black (%) 16.6 18.5 14.1 17.5 American Indian/Pacific Islander, Asian and Other (%) 15.9 21.1 11.2 10.3 Missing (%) 0.4 0.2 0.8 0 Hispanic ethnicity (%) 10.9 15.2 6.6 7.9 <0.0001 Rural residence (%) 35.4 35.6 35.4 34.1 0.952 Distance from the children’s hospital (mean), miles 31.3 (30.4) 31.7 (29.4) 31.1 (31.2) 30.5 (32.2) 0.896 Open in a new tab * Chi-square tests for categorical variables and t-tests for continuous variables comparing audio, video, and mixed groups Appointment outcomes for the entire study cohort For the entire cohort, outcomes of appointments were as follows: 57.2% completed, 34.4% canceled, and 8.4% missed. Completion rates for audio, video, and in-person visits were 80%, 71% and 37% respectively. Cancelation rates for audio, video, and in-person visits were 12%, 17% and 56%; and missed appointment rates were 8%, 12%, and 7% respectively. In the statistical model comparing appointment cancelation vs. completion ( Table 2 ), the odds of completion compared to cancelation was 10 times higher for an audio encounter and 6 times higher for video encounter compared to in-person encounters. Age, race, ethnicity, distance, and rural vs. urban residence were not significant in the cancelation vs. completed model. Table 2. Multivariable models comparing canceled vs. completed and missed vs. completed neurology visits for children Model Variables Canceled vs. Completed (n=1,212) Missed vs. Completed (n=1,206) Estimate Odds Ratio (95% CI) p value Estimate Odds Ratio (95% CI) p value Age 0.006 1.01 (0.99, 1.02) 0.42 0.008 1.01 (0.98, 1.03) 0.52 Race (White) * Black −0.012 0.99 (0.78, 1.25) 0.92 0.457 1.58 (1.14, 2.19) <0.01 Other 0.027 1.03 (0.76, 1.40) 0.86 −0.040 0.96 (0.62, 1.5) 0.86 Ethnicity (Non-Hispanic) * Hispanic −0.143 0.87 (0.62, 1.22) 0.41 0.068 1.07 (0.64, 1.79) 0.80 Health Insurance (Public) * Private 0.051 1.05 (0.89, 1.25) 0.56 −0.770 0.46 (0.34, 0.64) <0.0001 Residence (Urban) * Rural 0.037 1.04 (0.88, 1.22) 0.66 0.066 1.07 (0.79, 1.44) 0.67 Distance −0.002 1.00 (0.995, 1.0) 0.05 −0.011 0.989 (0.984, 0.995) <0.001 Appointment Type (In-Person) * Audio −2.313 0.1 (0.08, 0.12) <0.0001 −0.616 0.54 (0.40, 0.74) <0.0001 Video −1.840 0.16 (0.13, 0.19) <0.0001 0.062 1.06 (0.80, 1.41) 0.67 Open in a new tab * Reference category In the missed vs. completed appointment model, race, health insurance, distance from BCH, and type of encounter were significant variables. Children of Black race compared with those of White race, and those with public insurance compared to private insurance were twice as likely to have appointments missed rather than completed. With increasing distance from BCH, the odds of missed appointment decreased. Audio visits were twice as likely to be completed (vs. missed), whereas video visits were not different from in-person visits. Age, ethnicity and rural/urban residence were not significant variables in the missed vs. completion model. Appointment outcomes in children of Black race Next, we did a subgroup analysis of appointment outcomes for 207 children of Black race. Of the 627 appointments scheduled for these children, completion rates for audio, video and in-person visits were 78.1%, 59.3%, and 36.3% respectively. Cancelation rates for audio, video, and in-person visits were 12.5%, 16.4% and 51.9%; and missed appointment rates were 9.4%, 24.3%, and 11.9% respectively. We did multivariable analyses of appointment outcomes for children of Black race ( Table 3 ). In the completion vs, cancelation model, audio visits were 9 times, and video visits 5 times as likely as in-person visits to be completed rather than canceled. In the missed vs. completion model, audio visits were 3 times as likely as in-person visits to be completed vs. missed. There was no difference between video visits compared with in-person visits in missed appointments after adjusting for other variables in the model. Table 3. Multivariable Models of Neurology Appointment Outcomes for Children of Black Race Model Variables Canceled vs. Completed (n=199) Missed vs. Completed (n=202) Estimate Odds Ratio (95% CI) p value Estimate Odds Ratio (95% CI) p value Age 0.0002 1.00 (0.96, 1.04) 0.99 −0.007 0.99 (0.94, 1.04) 0.81 Health Insurance (Public) * Private 0.176 1.19 (0.75, 1.89) 0.46 −0.494 0.61 (0.28, 1.33) 0.22 Residence (Urban) * Rural 0.375 1.45 (0.82, 2.58) 0.20 0.023 1.02 (0.38, 2.77) 0.96 Distance −0.003 0.997 (0.987, 1.01) 0.54 −0.012 0.99 (0.97, 1.01) 0.17 Appointment Type (In-Person) * Audio −2.194 0.11 (0.07, 0.18) <0.0001 −0.960 0.38 (0.21, 0.72) <0.01 Video −1.639 0.19 (0.12, 0.31) <0.0001 0.235 1.26 (0.71, 2.24) 0.42 Open in a new tab * Reference category DISCUSSION Our study results point to the potential usefulness of audio visits in mitigating access problems for children needing outpatient neurology services. If flexibilities for audio visits were not an option during the pandemic, 59% of children in our study cohort would not have had pediatric neurology access for at least one appointment; 16% would not have had any pediatric neurology access. Our results show that audio visits are especially helpful for increasing access to pediatric neurology appointments for children of Black race. We found that that both audio and video appointments, compared to in-person visits, are less likely to be canceled. However, only audio appointments are less likely to be missed when compared with in-person encounters. While there is support for telehealth use for pediatric neurology clinic visits, 9 , 13 , 24 , 25 there is no consensus on which telehealth modality is best suited for different conditions or aspects of pediatric neurology care provision. 9 , 10 , 26 Patients seen in pediatric neurology clinics may require frequent follow-up visits, typically for medication management for epilepsy, headaches, tic disorders or other chronic conditions. However, for these types of visits, video assessments may not inherently be necessary. The best approach may be a shared decision-making model, wherein providers and families together decide on the best type of outpatient visit (audio vs. video vs. in-person) for the child given the type of care needed. Health systems caring for children should create structures and processes (such as clinic schedules and workflow) so that telehealth services remain an option for children for outpatient visits beyond the pandemic. Canceled or missed appointments contribute to burden on providers and health systems as well. 27 Hence, flexibilities in the type of appointments offered (audio, video, or in-person) could reduce the strain on the health system by reducing canceled or missed appointments. Consistent with prior studies, 11 , 13 , 14 our study shows that children of vulnerable groups – Black race, Hispanic ethnicity, and public insurance – are more likely to use audio visits than video visits. We also found that Black race and public insurance to be significant factors in missed appointments. Prior studies have noted Black patients have higher missed clinic appointments. 19 , 20 Many factors including economic and logistic factors, mistrust in the healthcare system, and systemic racism have been attributed to these differences. 10 , 14 , 28 – 30 In our study, audio visits reduced the odds of missed appointments for all children; this association was more pronounced for children of Black race. Similar moderating effect was not seen with video visits. Thus, our study provides evidence for the helpfulness of audio visits in reducing missed appointments for children, especially for those of Black race. Prior studies have shown that telehealth visit completion rates were higher for Black patients compared to White patients. 14 Thus, telehealth (especially audio encounter) has the potential to address some of the existing inequities in healthcare access. As states are evaluating discontinuation of telehealth flexibilities offered during the pandemic, policy makers should consider the effect of reversing these flexibilities in worsening disparities in children’s access to specialty care. Additionally, continued lower reimbursement for audio visits compared to video visits could negatively affect access to specialty services for children belonging to vulnerable groups. Our study has certain limitations. EHR data were limited to a single institution and not generalizable to other institutions or regions. However, our study findings are consistent with pediatric and adult studies conducted in other regions of the country. Since data was obtained from one institution’s EHR, we could have missed children’s clinical encounters elsewhere. Also, we did not obtain detailed information about the encounter (e.g. initial vs. follow-up, diagnoses associated with the encounter etc.) and could not determine how telehealth type varied by appointment characteristics. One of our study objectives was to understand the effect of audio visit on appointment outcomes specifically for children of Black race; however, there are well-established challenges in using race and ethnicity variables in EHR data. 31 Since this study used secondary data, we did not have other socio-demographic variables that could have affected appointment outcomes, such as transportation, internet access, or ability to access EHR portal. We did not collect data specifically for research purposes. Hence, it is possible that there were unmeasured confounders. Finally, since this is an observational study, the association between the type of telehealth and appointment outcomes do not indicate causation. CONCLUSION Telehealth use mitigated the problems with access to neurology services for children during the pandemic. The availability of audio visits was particularly helpful to improve access to neurology services for children of Black race. Reducing the availability of audio visits when pandemic telehealth flexibilities are reversed risks worsening access to specialty services for children in this subgroup. Acknowledgement: We thank Kwabena Sekyere (Programmer), Brian Ostasiewski (IT Program Director) and Larisa Rodgers (Lead Project Manager) of WF CTSI for their assistance with data abstraction and management. Funding Support: The project described was supported by the Wake Forest Clinical and Translational Science Institute (WF CTSI) Pilot Grant (PI: Nageswaran) as part of the CTSI Pilot Grant Program and the Wake Forest Center for Biomedical Informatics (WFBMI) funded by the National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (NCATS), National Institutes of Health, through Grant Award Number UL1TR001420. The content is solely the responsibility of the authors and does not necessarily represent the official views of the NIH. Abbreviations: BCH Brenner Children’s Hospital AHWFB Atrium Health Wake Forest Baptist EHR Electronic Health Record Footnotes Declaration of Interest: None Disclosure: A part of this paper was submitted as Abstract, and accepted for Poster Presentation at the Child Neurology Society Annual Meeting to be held in October 2022. REFERENCES 1. SPECIAL BULLETIN COVID-19 #34: Telehealth Clinical Policy Modifications – Definitions, Eligible Providers, Services and Codes. 2020; https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/blog/2020/04/07/special-bulletin-covid-19-34-telehealth-clinical-policy-modifications-%E2%80%93-definitions . Accessed 4/28/22. 2. Uscher-Pines L, McCullough C, Dworsky MS, et al. Use of Telehealth Across Pediatric Subspecialties Before and During the COVID-19 Pandemic. JAMA Netw Open. 2022;5(3):e224759. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Muppavarapu 